00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:31.660
Now, I wanna start by saying

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:33.890
there was no field of
quantum information science

00:07:33.890 --> 00:07:35.640
when I got my PhD,

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:38.860
and to illustrate how long ago that was,

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:42.150
this is a picture of me

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:45.890
on the MIT Physics Department soccer team,

00:07:45.890 --> 00:07:48.660
that skinny guy with lots of hair.

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:51.003
So you can see that that was a while ago.

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:53.950
The things that were being studied then

00:07:53.950 --> 00:07:58.950
were the Higgs boson,
gravitational waves, dark matter,

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:00.910
and string theory.

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:04.030
So all these cutting edge
things you've heard about,

00:08:04.030 --> 00:08:06.430
even more recently, in physics,

00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:08.550
were actually already targets

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:11.940
for big chunks of the physics community

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:14.510
and astrophysics, et cetera then,

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:16.750
but not quantum information science.

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:19.430
Quantum information science didn't exist,

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:22.480
but the precursors were people who worried

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:25.833
about the philosophical
foundations of quantum mechanics,

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:28.530
that worried about the measurement problem

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:29.920
in quantum mechanics

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:32.650
and a little bit about irreversibility

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:34.100
in the direction of time,

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:36.400
very much fringe activities

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:39.663
that weren't considered real physics.

00:08:40.790 --> 00:08:42.970
Okay, well, now, of course,

00:08:42.970 --> 00:08:47.787
this is accepted as maybe the
most exciting frontier area

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:52.180
in physical science

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:55.950
and with the connection to
computer science and mathematics,

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:58.560
and it just came out of a left field

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:00.070
in a way that I'm going to describe.

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:02.080
So you can call that

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:04.480
scientific revolution or transformative,

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:06.560
but I think it's the most surprising

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:10.330
and exciting development in
physics in the last 50 years,

00:09:10.330 --> 00:09:12.620
in my humble opinion.

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:15.840
So I felt we should try
to engage the public

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:17.810
about this quantum information science

00:09:17.810 --> 00:09:19.330
because it is so novel

00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:21.713
and it could be quite important as well.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:23.770
So the outline of my talk

00:09:23.770 --> 00:09:25.990
is I'm gonna begin with
a historical context.

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:28.620
Of course, this is sort of my thing.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:30.240
Quantum mechanics was invented

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:32.590
as the theory of the microscopic world,

00:09:32.590 --> 00:09:36.510
atoms, molecules nuclei, the
origin of light and heat,

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:40.663
and it was developed
between about 1900 and 1930,

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:44.080
and there were conceptual challenges

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:45.990
with the new quantum theory

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:49.620
related to the idea of
superposition and entanglement

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:53.210
of the effect of measurement
and a changing paradigm

00:09:53.210 --> 00:09:55.353
for what existed in nature,

00:09:56.190 --> 00:10:00.870
but in the maturing period
between 1930 and 1980

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:02.870
there was great success,

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:06.580
but constraints on what
we could actually predict

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:08.993
from quantum theory that
I'm gonna talk about,

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:12.960
and it was only in 1980s

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:15.570
that this radical fringe movement formed

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:19.300
that began to study quantum
information science,

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:22.853
and then in the 1990s
there was a Eureka moment,

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:28.620
which created this worldwide
race to perfect the technology

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:30.850
and specifically to
build a quantum computer,

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:32.720
which I'm gonna talk about,

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:35.250
and then since 2000,

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:39.540
there's been an effort to
actually make this a reality.

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:42.270
The early work was all theoretical work,

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:43.753
mainly theoretical work,

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:47.900
and actually the quantum
computer is a new kind of machine

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:50.150
unlike any of the others we built,

00:10:50.150 --> 00:10:52.380
even using quantum
mechanics to build them,

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:56.330
and I'm gonna explain why it's
such a new kind of machine,

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:57.163
and then finally,

00:10:57.163 --> 00:11:00.840
I'll briefly talk about the
social and economic impact

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:04.543
of quantum information
science and technology.

00:11:07.180 --> 00:11:12.073
So, in 1900, the exploration
of inner space began.

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:14.640
Through the 19th Century

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:17.800
physicists and chemists had hypothesized

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:20.240
that they were smallest constituents

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:22.550
of matter, atoms and molecules

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:25.370
and were able to explain
the property of gases

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:27.390
and some of the properties of heat

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:30.130
and the flow of heat
using this point of view,

00:11:30.130 --> 00:11:33.000
and during that period
the working assumption

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:38.000
was that the same laws of
physics, of Newtonian physics

00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:42.860
and the electromagnetism
of Maxwell and Faraday

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:45.340
would apply also to these atoms

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:48.270
possibly with new forces
that we would discover

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:51.673
and that turned out not
at all to be the case.

00:11:52.691 --> 00:11:55.760
So, and this word quantum arose

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:57.697
and that's the first question one asks.

00:11:57.697 --> 00:12:01.020
Why is it called quantum mechanics?

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:06.020
And it was introduced
by Max Planck in 1900

00:12:07.510 --> 00:12:12.510
and he was trying to explain
the vibrations of molecules,

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:16.880
which both act as a
microscopic source of heat

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:19.940
and also as a source of thermal radiation,

00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:22.760
and he was trying to
explain the observations

00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:24.520
of this thermal radiation

00:12:24.520 --> 00:12:26.940
and he was able to explain that

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:31.140
only by assuming that the
vibrational energy was quantized

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:33.380
and the smallest unit epsilon,

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:35.910
we physicist are contractually required

00:12:35.910 --> 00:12:40.910
to use Greek letters here
to make it sound more fancy.

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:44.190
So epsilon is the smallest unit of energy.

00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:47.240
Nu is the frequency of the vibration,

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:49.560
and h has this new constant
that Planck discovered

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:50.880
called Planck constant,

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:53.010
and because of this quantization

00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:55.010
this vibrating molecule was assumed

00:12:55.010 --> 00:12:57.730
to only be able to take certain energies

00:12:57.730 --> 00:13:00.750
equal to an integer number times h nu,

00:13:00.750 --> 00:13:03.910
and this is may not

00:13:03.910 --> 00:13:06.720
immediately strike you
as being totally crazy,

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:09.350
but it is totally crazy
in a certain sense.

00:13:09.350 --> 00:13:13.340
So the macroscopic analog
of this simple oscillator

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:15.490
would be a pendulum, right?

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:18.150
And when the pendulum is just facing down,

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:19.800
let's say it has its minimum energy,

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:23.550
just potential energy, no kinetic energy,

00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:26.050
and then when I displace
it and let it move

00:13:26.050 --> 00:13:29.170
it gets kinetic energy along
with the potential energy,

00:13:29.170 --> 00:13:31.810
and the more I displace it

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:34.720
the more kinetic energy it's going to get.

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:36.640
So it looks like the energy

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:39.690
that I can give to this oscillator

00:13:39.690 --> 00:13:41.770
is related to the continuity of space

00:13:41.770 --> 00:13:43.570
and must be continuous

00:13:43.570 --> 00:13:46.670
'cause it seems like I
could display by any amount,

00:13:46.670 --> 00:13:49.377
but the implication of Planck's hypothesis

00:13:49.377 --> 00:13:51.180
was that that's not the case

00:13:51.180 --> 00:13:54.520
that actually only certain magnitudes

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:56.790
or amplitudes of vibration were allowed,

00:13:56.790 --> 00:14:00.860
something totally outside
of Newtonian physics.

00:14:00.860 --> 00:14:03.680
Now, the young Einstein showed up

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:05.990
and he contributed to this.

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:08.920
Planck had not embraced his hypothesis

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:12.330
as a new law of physics
that would change mechanics,

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:14.450
but Einstein very much did

00:14:14.450 --> 00:14:18.140
and in 1907 he wrote the following words,

00:14:18.140 --> 00:14:20.070
for although one has thought before

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:23.110
that the motion of molecules
obeys the same laws

00:14:23.110 --> 00:14:24.750
that hold for the motion of bodies

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:26.750
in our world of sense perception,

00:14:26.750 --> 00:14:29.760
we must now assume that the
energy of elementary structures

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:34.630
can only assume the values
zero, h nu, two h nu, et cetera.

00:14:34.630 --> 00:14:38.670
Okay, now, related to
this he had introduced...

00:14:38.670 --> 00:14:39.830
So this was, as I said,

00:14:39.830 --> 00:14:42.400
a radical violation of Newtonian mechanics

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:44.160
and it hints at a coming revolution

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:46.260
which Einstein was embracing,

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:49.660
and in fact, in 1905,
earlier he had proposed

00:14:49.660 --> 00:14:51.480
thinking about Planck's work

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:54.220
that actually that light itself

00:14:54.220 --> 00:14:56.870
could have a particulate behavior

00:14:56.870 --> 00:14:58.170
and that could be thought

00:14:58.170 --> 00:15:02.050
of as consisting of little
particles each with energy h nu,

00:15:02.050 --> 00:15:04.383
which we now call the photon.

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:10.280
So, and that introduced this strange idea

00:15:10.580 --> 00:15:14.700
that light could at once
be a wave and a particle

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:17.070
because light interferes with itself

00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:22.070
and we had a theory of light
waves, not of light particles.

00:15:23.740 --> 00:15:27.440
So, Einstein eventually embraced the idea

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:29.560
that there was going to be a new theory

00:15:29.560 --> 00:15:31.220
with wave-particle duality,

00:15:31.220 --> 00:15:32.980
which is indeed what happens.

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:34.430
Now, one of the interesting things

00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:38.790
is that quantization appears
somewhat naturally for waves

00:15:38.790 --> 00:15:40.680
when they're confined, okay?

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:44.170
So for example if I tie
a string at two points

00:15:44.170 --> 00:15:46.910
and I vibrate it,

00:15:46.910 --> 00:15:48.550
then all of its vibrations

00:15:48.550 --> 00:15:52.640
can be reduced to certain
quantize vibrations

00:15:52.640 --> 00:15:55.320
with a fundamental half wavelength

00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:58.170
and then the next overtone
with a full wavelength

00:15:58.170 --> 00:16:00.070
and then three halves and so on

00:16:00.070 --> 00:16:01.670
and this is the harmonic series

00:16:01.670 --> 00:16:04.183
that a lot of people know in music,

00:16:05.090 --> 00:16:06.780
and there's a natural quantization.

00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:09.000
You cannot have frequencies

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:11.860
in between these allowed harmonics.

00:16:11.860 --> 00:16:14.300
So if there was wave-particle duality

00:16:14.300 --> 00:16:17.007
maybe that could lead to quantization.

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:20.690
Now actually, Einstein
focused his research

00:16:20.690 --> 00:16:21.980
mainly on quantum theory,

00:16:21.980 --> 00:16:25.410
not in relativity between 1907 and 1911,

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:28.870
and by the end of 1910 he gave up

00:16:28.870 --> 00:16:33.870
and he wrote, the riddle of
radiation will not yield.

00:16:34.230 --> 00:16:38.550
So, but just at that time actually,

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:41.440
all the other people who
had not quite recognized

00:16:41.440 --> 00:16:43.940
the looming crisis in physics

00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:45.480
they realized there was a crisis.

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:48.530
There was this famous
first Solvay Conference

00:16:48.530 --> 00:16:50.460
in November 1911

00:16:50.460 --> 00:16:53.870
on the topic of radiation and quanta,

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:57.050
and there you see, Mr. Solvay,

00:16:57.050 --> 00:17:00.393
this wealthy industrialist
who paid for this,

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:05.740
not unlike events now at the
Aspen Institute, et cetara,

00:17:05.740 --> 00:17:08.550
and there's Einstein,

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:10.900
but actually he was very disappointed

00:17:10.900 --> 00:17:13.830
because he'd already run into a brick wall

00:17:13.830 --> 00:17:17.000
and he wrote to his friend De Calabaso

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:20.380
that the conference was
like the Lamentations

00:17:20.380 --> 00:17:22.640
on the ruins of Jerusalem.

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:24.610
I did not find it very stimulating

00:17:24.610 --> 00:17:27.883
because I did not hear anything
that I did not already know.

00:17:29.196 --> 00:17:32.720
So the next person then
to really make progress

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:33.900
was Niels Bohr,

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:35.010
who actually found out

00:17:35.010 --> 00:17:37.840
about the Solvay
Conference from Rutherford

00:17:38.890 --> 00:17:42.050
and he introduced this model of an atom,

00:17:42.050 --> 00:17:43.990
where again you had quantization,

00:17:43.990 --> 00:17:47.540
but this was quantization in
terms of different orbits.

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:50.580
Now, it was known by that time

00:17:50.580 --> 00:17:52.650
that the atom consisted of a nucleus

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:54.350
which was positively charged,

00:17:54.350 --> 00:17:55.800
and negative electrons,

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:56.730
and you could think of them

00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:59.950
orbiting in a kind of solar system model,

00:17:59.950 --> 00:18:02.210
but of course, in classical physics,

00:18:02.210 --> 00:18:04.130
the orbits could have any radius,

00:18:04.130 --> 00:18:09.130
but or proposed with some
justification, some argument,

00:18:09.290 --> 00:18:10.660
not completely rigorous,

00:18:10.660 --> 00:18:14.100
that only certain orbits would be allowed

00:18:14.100 --> 00:18:15.840
and the only thing that could happen

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:18.530
is that an electron could
jump between these orbits

00:18:18.530 --> 00:18:22.433
emitting energy h nu in the form of light,

00:18:23.290 --> 00:18:24.500
and the interesting thing

00:18:24.500 --> 00:18:26.890
is he could calculate these energy levels

00:18:26.890 --> 00:18:28.540
and they were not equally spaced

00:18:28.540 --> 00:18:30.430
like this harmonic oscillator

00:18:30.430 --> 00:18:32.070
that we were just talking about

00:18:32.070 --> 00:18:34.230
and so this spacing of the energy levels

00:18:34.230 --> 00:18:38.200
depends a little bit on the
nature of the forces involved

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:43.200
and the equals h nu was just
true for harmonic oscillator

00:18:44.990 --> 00:18:46.970
and then we knew how to calculate

00:18:46.970 --> 00:18:49.010
the energy levels of atoms.

00:18:49.010 --> 00:18:52.210
Okay, and because you
have different spacings,

00:18:52.210 --> 00:18:54.410
then that means different
frequencies of light

00:18:54.410 --> 00:18:55.920
can be emitted from the atom,

00:18:55.920 --> 00:18:57.623
but they're discrete frequencies,

00:18:58.960 --> 00:19:01.580
but this orbit picture
turned out to be wrong.

00:19:01.580 --> 00:19:03.250
It took 13 more years

00:19:03.250 --> 00:19:06.370
to really come up with a
more quantitative theory,

00:19:06.370 --> 00:19:08.410
which is essentially the modern theory.

00:19:08.410 --> 00:19:13.107
This was done mainly by
Heisenberg and Erwin Schrodinger,

00:19:13.107 --> 00:19:15.600
and they replace this orbit picture

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:18.290
with the picture of clouds of probability.

00:19:18.290 --> 00:19:20.010
There was this idea

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:23.860
that a state for an atom
would be not in orbit

00:19:23.860 --> 00:19:27.280
and that the electron actually could be

00:19:27.280 --> 00:19:29.560
in some sort of cloudy region

00:19:29.560 --> 00:19:34.560
with a particular kind
of variation of the cloud

00:19:34.640 --> 00:19:37.620
depending on how much energy it had.

00:19:37.620 --> 00:19:39.780
It's related to this thing
called a wave function,

00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:42.380
we'll be talking about a little bit,

00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:45.080
and in this case the
position of the electron

00:19:45.080 --> 00:19:46.830
in the wave function is uncertain.

00:19:46.830 --> 00:19:49.280
You'll find it in different
places when you measure

00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:50.940
for the same state,

00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:52.770
but if you measure the energy

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:55.190
you'll always get the quantized energy,

00:19:55.190 --> 00:19:56.890
and in this talk

00:19:56.890 --> 00:20:00.270
and for what we're gonna do
in quantum information today,

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:03.630
I'm gonna mostly focus on
variables that are discrete,

00:20:03.630 --> 00:20:07.553
like the energy here that can
only take quantized values.

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:12.243
Okay, so we're moving right
along with quantum mechanics.

00:20:16.771 --> 00:20:19.410
So one thing that followed from the theory

00:20:19.410 --> 00:20:22.560
that was invented in '25, '26 '27,

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:25.740
was that when an atom was emitting light

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:27.450
through this quantum jump,

00:20:27.450 --> 00:20:31.340
that it could actually be
in between the two states,

00:20:31.340 --> 00:20:35.430
in a state called a superposition
which we write this way.

00:20:35.430 --> 00:20:37.660
So psi is this wave function

00:20:37.660 --> 00:20:41.810
and it's proportional to an
equal sum of the two states,

00:20:41.810 --> 00:20:44.273
and I'm being very vague
mathematically here,

00:20:45.120 --> 00:20:48.050
but the meaning of the
superposition, at least partially,

00:20:48.050 --> 00:20:51.550
was that when you measure
the energy of the electron

00:20:51.550 --> 00:20:53.120
in a superposition,

00:20:53.120 --> 00:20:58.120
50% of the time you would
get the energy level E2

00:20:58.400 --> 00:21:01.273
and 50% of the time you
get the energy level E3.

00:21:03.420 --> 00:21:05.900
Okay, and you also can have
unequal superpositions,

00:21:05.900 --> 00:21:08.450
like you could have
something with a half there

00:21:08.450 --> 00:21:12.810
and that would correspond to
finding E2 75% of the time

00:21:12.810 --> 00:21:16.150
and E3 25% of the time.

00:21:16.150 --> 00:21:19.970
Okay, so so far maybe
that's not too upsetting.

00:21:19.970 --> 00:21:24.040
Now, to talk about this in
an information theoretic way,

00:21:24.040 --> 00:21:29.040
we're gonna replace the two
levels by a zero and a one,

00:21:29.710 --> 00:21:31.390
as in the states of a bit,

00:21:31.390 --> 00:21:33.520
which we're gonna call a quantum bit,

00:21:33.520 --> 00:21:38.290
but this is a bit that can be
in this superposition state

00:21:38.290 --> 00:21:40.110
and while every quantum system

00:21:40.110 --> 00:21:43.900
has usually many energy
levels or an infinite number,

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:46.350
in actual using it for
information processing

00:21:46.350 --> 00:21:49.690
we're often gonna focus on
just two levels or a few,

00:21:49.690 --> 00:21:53.080
and so we talk about this quantum bit

00:21:53.080 --> 00:21:55.030
as if there were only two levels

00:21:55.030 --> 00:21:57.340
until the other levels start to come in

00:21:57.340 --> 00:22:00.310
and then we'll move away from that,

00:22:00.310 --> 00:22:02.960
but mainly, we're gonna
talk about quantum bits.

00:22:02.960 --> 00:22:06.120
Now, measuring a quantum
bit in superposition,

00:22:06.120 --> 00:22:09.450
is this just the same as measuring a coin

00:22:09.450 --> 00:22:11.490
that's been tossed inside a box, right?

00:22:11.490 --> 00:22:15.100
So let's say it's a 50, 50 superposition.

00:22:15.100 --> 00:22:17.620
So I toss a coin inside a box,

00:22:17.620 --> 00:22:19.590
I don't look at it yet

00:22:19.590 --> 00:22:20.910
and then when I open it up

00:22:20.910 --> 00:22:23.760
I have a 50% chance of getting tails,

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:28.498
or I do it again, 50%
chance of having heads.

00:22:28.498 --> 00:22:31.470
So, and then an unequal superposition

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:33.790
could be just like a biased coin,

00:22:33.790 --> 00:22:35.690
which is somehow loaded to come up

00:22:35.690 --> 00:22:37.343
more like heads and tails,

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:40.940
and more heads than tails.

00:22:40.940 --> 00:22:43.930
So where's the big philosophical
problem with a bias coin?

00:22:43.930 --> 00:22:47.840
I mean, it doesn't sound
like a total showstopper.

00:22:47.840 --> 00:22:51.610
Okay, well, here's where it
starts to get more challenging.

00:22:51.610 --> 00:22:55.263
Okay, so I do this, I don't look at it,

00:22:57.670 --> 00:22:59.940
and then same thing, I do it again.

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:03.880
I get some value of heads or tails,

00:23:03.880 --> 00:23:06.200
but when I finally opened the box

00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:09.820
I'm able to assume that
before I opened the box

00:23:09.820 --> 00:23:12.570
it had the value before
I actually could see it,

00:23:12.570 --> 00:23:14.580
quote unquote, measure.

00:23:14.580 --> 00:23:17.140
So the inability to predict heads or tails

00:23:17.140 --> 00:23:20.580
when I toss a coin is
a matter of ignorance.

00:23:20.580 --> 00:23:23.550
I don't know what it is
if I don't look at it,

00:23:23.550 --> 00:23:26.130
but it was that before I mentioned.

00:23:26.130 --> 00:23:30.129
So measurement just reveals the
state that was there before,

00:23:30.129 --> 00:23:32.510
and you can't say this for quantum bits.

00:23:32.510 --> 00:23:34.220
It really is not true

00:23:35.395 --> 00:23:38.310
and so far as physics
can talk about truth.

00:23:38.310 --> 00:23:40.730
So that's where things
start to get complicated

00:23:40.730 --> 00:23:43.120
and now I'll try to explain why.

00:23:43.120 --> 00:23:47.700
Okay, so the usual coin would be 50, 50,

00:23:47.700 --> 00:23:49.763
we're gonna call it zero in one state,

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:52.660
but you know if it's biased

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:54.280
I could have a totally loaded coin

00:23:54.280 --> 00:23:56.700
that's completely always comes up head

00:23:56.700 --> 00:23:58.430
or always comes up tails,

00:23:58.430 --> 00:24:00.070
or I could have anything in between,

00:24:00.070 --> 00:24:04.730
say, comes up two thirds of
tails and one thirds head.

00:24:04.730 --> 00:24:05.680
There's nothing surprising,

00:24:05.680 --> 00:24:09.130
and all the different kinds
of probabilistic behavior

00:24:09.130 --> 00:24:10.653
are on this line here,

00:24:11.892 --> 00:24:15.270
but qubit states are not like that, okay?

00:24:15.270 --> 00:24:17.980
This equal superposition I showed you

00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:20.830
is like the 50, 50 unbiased coin,

00:24:20.830 --> 00:24:24.150
but I can write a different quantum state

00:24:24.150 --> 00:24:28.200
where I put a minus sign
in front of the state one

00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:30.930
and that'll also be 50,
50 when I measure it,

00:24:30.930 --> 00:24:33.120
and then there are nastier
ones that I'm not showing you

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:34.770
that involve complex numbers

00:24:34.770 --> 00:24:38.150
and in fact, there's actually
an infinity of unbiased coins

00:24:38.150 --> 00:24:39.840
and the states of a quantum bit

00:24:43.070 --> 00:24:44.650
can't be described by a line,

00:24:44.650 --> 00:24:47.860
they have to be described by this sphere

00:24:47.860 --> 00:24:52.110
where always having one,
the totally biased coin,

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:55.530
is the two poles of the sphere, okay?

00:24:55.530 --> 00:24:57.740
And the two states I just wrote here

00:25:02.991 --> 00:25:05.210
would be on the equator of the sphere,

00:25:05.210 --> 00:25:07.270
and the equator is a circle,

00:25:07.270 --> 00:25:09.730
and so there's a continuum
of unbiased states

00:25:09.730 --> 00:25:11.380
all of which are distinct,

00:25:11.380 --> 00:25:14.570
and then when you measure the energy,

00:25:14.570 --> 00:25:18.060
they could still gonna be 50, 50, okay.

00:25:18.060 --> 00:25:21.230
After you measure the
energy, if you get one,

00:25:21.230 --> 00:25:25.890
it's gonna stay one till something
else that manipulates it.

00:25:25.890 --> 00:25:27.470
So the measurement has changed,

00:25:27.470 --> 00:25:29.390
the state is now completely one

00:25:29.390 --> 00:25:32.020
even though it started out in that state,

00:25:32.020 --> 00:25:34.413
that was 50, 50, one and zero, okay.

00:25:35.680 --> 00:25:38.790
So what makes all these
different unbiased states

00:25:38.790 --> 00:25:42.730
on the equator of this
sphere different states?

00:25:42.730 --> 00:25:44.140
Well, you can tell they're different

00:25:44.140 --> 00:25:47.220
when you measure something
else, which is not the energy,

00:25:47.220 --> 00:25:49.430
and to keep life simple we're gonna assume

00:25:49.430 --> 00:25:52.800
you measure something, which is discrete

00:25:52.800 --> 00:25:54.650
and could just be zero or one,

00:25:54.650 --> 00:25:56.210
but a different meaning of zero or one.

00:25:56.210 --> 00:25:57.830
Some other physical property

00:25:57.830 --> 00:26:00.330
like momentum or something
else, angular momentum.

00:26:01.720 --> 00:26:05.180
Now, these states on the
equator won't be unbiased

00:26:05.180 --> 00:26:07.230
when you measure this other property,

00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:08.800
and the bias will not be the same.

00:26:08.800 --> 00:26:10.330
So they're not the same state

00:26:12.966 --> 00:26:16.940
and they don't map on to just
a biased or unbiased coin.

00:26:16.940 --> 00:26:19.910
Okay, so do we really have to accept

00:26:19.910 --> 00:26:22.370
that the state is uncertain
until we measure it?

00:26:22.370 --> 00:26:24.240
Could we still interpret our observations

00:26:24.240 --> 00:26:26.010
as learning what was already there?

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:28.570
Which is certainly was
from the very beginning

00:26:28.570 --> 00:26:33.030
the question that people ask

00:26:33.030 --> 00:26:35.280
because they wanted to think of physics

00:26:35.280 --> 00:26:37.963
as objective reality
independent measurement.

00:26:39.310 --> 00:26:43.143
So the answer is no, we can't do that.

00:26:44.210 --> 00:26:45.710
It seems like in quantum mechanics

00:26:45.710 --> 00:26:47.130
measurement is affecting the system

00:26:47.130 --> 00:26:49.570
and making it choose properties.

00:26:49.570 --> 00:26:51.540
The properties are not independent

00:26:51.540 --> 00:26:53.660
of the actual measurement,

00:26:53.660 --> 00:26:57.400
and Bohr respond to this
by saying get over it.

00:26:57.400 --> 00:26:59.670
This is known more formally

00:26:59.670 --> 00:27:04.280
as the Copenhagen interpretation
or complementarity,

00:27:04.280 --> 00:27:06.500
and he just says your
choice of measurements

00:27:06.500 --> 00:27:08.140
determines what you can know

00:27:08.140 --> 00:27:10.830
and there are questions
that don't have an answer,

00:27:10.830 --> 00:27:13.740
you shouldn't expect them to have answers.

00:27:13.740 --> 00:27:16.110
Later it was actually
proved that any new theory

00:27:16.110 --> 00:27:18.530
which assumes properties of quantum bits,

00:27:18.530 --> 00:27:20.490
they're independent of
how they're measured,

00:27:20.490 --> 00:27:22.380
could never agree with all the predictions

00:27:22.380 --> 00:27:23.630
of quantum mechanics,

00:27:23.630 --> 00:27:25.343
and Bohr was, I'm fine with that.

00:27:26.300 --> 00:27:28.220
Okay, but not Einstein.

00:27:28.220 --> 00:27:31.500
So Einstein, famously in 1935,

00:27:31.500 --> 00:27:34.500
wrote this paper with two young colleagues

00:27:34.500 --> 00:27:36.860
at the Institute for Advanced Studies,

00:27:36.860 --> 00:27:39.620
called can quantum mechanical description

00:27:39.620 --> 00:27:42.750
of physical reality be
considered complete?

00:27:42.750 --> 00:27:47.750
And in it he did a thought
experiment, he liked those,

00:27:48.130 --> 00:27:50.040
in which he tried to argue

00:27:50.040 --> 00:27:52.300
that it can't be considered complete,

00:27:52.300 --> 00:27:56.660
and the reason that he argued,

00:27:56.660 --> 00:27:59.760
his argument was related to
the concept of entanglement.

00:27:59.760 --> 00:28:03.050
So entanglement is
again, I have two qubits,

00:28:03.050 --> 00:28:06.123
I don't know what their state is, okay?

00:28:07.340 --> 00:28:10.492
But I put them in a state
that has the property

00:28:10.492 --> 00:28:14.040
that if I get the two of them,
and they're in a joint state,

00:28:14.040 --> 00:28:17.440
and when I measure them,
if I know what one is,

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:20.460
I immediately find the other
one has to be the same.

00:28:20.460 --> 00:28:23.500
So their values are called entangle.

00:28:23.500 --> 00:28:26.670
Now each coin separately is unbiased,

00:28:26.670 --> 00:28:29.600
it just comes up 50, 50 as entails,

00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:32.210
but if I measure the second one,

00:28:32.210 --> 00:28:34.570
actually it has to be the same.

00:28:34.570 --> 00:28:38.760
These are these magic coins,
also known as EPR pairs,

00:28:38.760 --> 00:28:42.730
after Einstein hold on all scheme rose.

00:28:42.730 --> 00:28:44.650
So they look like this in our notation.

00:28:44.650 --> 00:28:46.990
So you either get one
and one or zero and zero,

00:28:46.990 --> 00:28:50.393
and you never get zero one or one zero.

00:28:53.310 --> 00:28:56.650
So each coin individually
carries no information

00:28:56.650 --> 00:28:58.410
because it's not biased,

00:28:58.410 --> 00:29:00.290
but the correlation does,

00:29:00.290 --> 00:29:02.630
the entanglement does carry information,

00:29:02.630 --> 00:29:04.650
because something about this.

00:29:04.650 --> 00:29:06.700
In this state, zero one will never happen

00:29:06.700 --> 00:29:08.200
and one zero will never happen.

00:29:08.200 --> 00:29:10.310
So you have one bit of information,

00:29:10.310 --> 00:29:13.840
but it's shared between the two bits.

00:29:13.840 --> 00:29:15.660
Now the thing that makes it even worse

00:29:15.660 --> 00:29:18.660
and that Einstein particularly
objected to is the following.

00:29:18.660 --> 00:29:21.970
Suppose I have these entangled state,

00:29:21.970 --> 00:29:23.630
and I take one of the bits

00:29:23.630 --> 00:29:25.910
and I let it fly off a great distance

00:29:25.910 --> 00:29:28.410
like a light year, or something like that

00:29:28.410 --> 00:29:31.870
and then I do the measurement
here and I find heads.

00:29:31.870 --> 00:29:35.000
Well, since you have to get heads

00:29:37.580 --> 00:29:40.270
on the other bit that's far away

00:29:40.270 --> 00:29:45.270
and if I measured it first
it would have been random.

00:29:47.080 --> 00:29:50.310
So it sort of feels like
this measurement here

00:29:50.310 --> 00:29:52.010
affected the measurement over there.

00:29:52.010 --> 00:29:54.900
Now it turns out you can prove
you can't send information

00:29:54.900 --> 00:29:55.870
with this correlations.

00:29:55.870 --> 00:29:57.780
So you can't go faster than light,

00:29:57.780 --> 00:30:02.400
but still it looks very
impossible or very wrong,

00:30:02.400 --> 00:30:06.720
and so how can a measurement
here affect the result there?

00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:08.590
Einstein said it can't.

00:30:08.590 --> 00:30:11.210
Sometimes he would make
fun of the whole idea

00:30:11.210 --> 00:30:14.510
of measurement affecting
reality by saying,

00:30:14.510 --> 00:30:16.220
do you really think the moon isn't there

00:30:16.220 --> 00:30:17.720
when you aren't looking at it?

00:30:20.020 --> 00:30:21.830
And he thought there
must be a better theory

00:30:21.830 --> 00:30:25.380
where only things near each coin

00:30:25.380 --> 00:30:26.980
determine what their value is,

00:30:26.980 --> 00:30:28.930
and somehow you can still explain

00:30:28.930 --> 00:30:30.900
the correlation that exists.

00:30:30.900 --> 00:30:35.000
So this brilliant young
theorist back in '64, John Bell,

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:37.710
actually took up Einstein's challenge.

00:30:37.710 --> 00:30:39.390
He said could there be a better theory?

00:30:39.390 --> 00:30:41.090
Is that even allowed?

00:30:41.090 --> 00:30:45.323
And he figured out the
following theorem, okay.

00:30:48.838 --> 00:30:52.510
So in his theory, he
calculated the correlation

00:30:52.510 --> 00:30:53.930
between two measurements of cubits,

00:30:53.930 --> 00:30:56.390
I'm not gonna go into exactly

00:30:56.390 --> 00:30:58.280
what kind of measurement that would be,

00:30:58.280 --> 00:31:02.030
but it's similar to checking
which state it's in,

00:31:02.030 --> 00:31:04.280
but it's a little more complicated,

00:31:04.280 --> 00:31:07.100
and he calculated how this correlation

00:31:07.100 --> 00:31:08.880
would vary with a certain angle

00:31:08.880 --> 00:31:10.430
that you vary in the measurement,

00:31:10.430 --> 00:31:13.900
and you see this blue
curve I'm calling quantum

00:31:13.900 --> 00:31:16.300
and that's the prediction

00:31:16.300 --> 00:31:18.960
that quantum mechanics
gives for this measurement

00:31:18.960 --> 00:31:21.690
and the red curve Bell established

00:31:21.690 --> 00:31:25.830
is the largest values you can have

00:31:25.830 --> 00:31:28.460
for any theory that Einstein would like,

00:31:28.460 --> 00:31:30.850
that only depends on the local variables,

00:31:30.850 --> 00:31:33.217
and so he said, "Just
go up and look at this

00:31:33.217 --> 00:31:36.307
"and see if we violate this inequality.

00:31:36.307 --> 00:31:39.307
"If we don't, then quantum
mechanics is wrong,

00:31:39.307 --> 00:31:41.717
"and Einstein friendly
theory could be there,

00:31:41.717 --> 00:31:42.787
"could be found,

00:31:42.787 --> 00:31:46.217
"but if we do violate this inequality,

00:31:46.217 --> 00:31:48.577
"then we could never
explain it with a theory

00:31:48.577 --> 00:31:50.260
"that Einstein would accept."

00:31:50.260 --> 00:31:52.800
And so that's the
difference between these two

00:31:52.800 --> 00:31:55.840
at this particular angle, this difference.

00:31:55.840 --> 00:31:58.230
If we see this Einstein is wrong,

00:31:58.230 --> 00:31:59.780
if we see oops,

00:31:59.780 --> 00:32:04.780
if we see the red then
maybe Einstein was right,

00:32:05.680 --> 00:32:09.760
and this young French
physicist Alain Aspect in '82

00:32:09.760 --> 00:32:11.880
did a very good experiment to test this.

00:32:11.880 --> 00:32:12.910
There were some earlier ones,

00:32:12.910 --> 00:32:14.600
but this certainly was
one of the very best

00:32:14.600 --> 00:32:16.040
first early ones,

00:32:16.040 --> 00:32:17.140
and there's his curve

00:32:17.140 --> 00:32:19.250
and you see it looks like the blue curve

00:32:19.250 --> 00:32:22.350
and it violates what are
called Bell's inequalities.

00:32:22.350 --> 00:32:24.740
So that means we'll never be able to get

00:32:24.740 --> 00:32:27.890
the kind of theory that
Einstein was looking for

00:32:27.890 --> 00:32:29.220
and we really have to accept

00:32:29.220 --> 00:32:31.730
these weird properties of entanglement,

00:32:31.730 --> 00:32:34.880
and really many, many
experiments have been done

00:32:34.880 --> 00:32:38.430
and this kind of thing has been
verified in different forums

00:32:38.430 --> 00:32:39.500
over and over again.

00:32:39.500 --> 00:32:42.860
So the consensus is that the
world is really this weird.

00:32:42.860 --> 00:32:44.900
Yes, the world is really that.

00:32:44.900 --> 00:32:48.310
Okay, now Bell really
was kind of upset by this

00:32:48.310 --> 00:32:51.490
and he wrote Einstein
was the rational man.

00:32:51.490 --> 00:32:54.840
The reasonable thing that he
thought just doesn't work.

00:32:54.840 --> 00:32:56.280
So he was hoping

00:32:56.280 --> 00:32:59.740
that maybe you'd find a problem
with the quantum prediction,

00:32:59.740 --> 00:33:00.693
but we didn't.

00:33:03.030 --> 00:33:04.840
Okay, so to summarize

00:33:04.840 --> 00:33:06.660
we've learned quantum mechanics, right?

00:33:06.660 --> 00:33:07.590
Sort of.

00:33:07.590 --> 00:33:10.430
Quantum systems can live
in an uncertain state

00:33:10.430 --> 00:33:12.060
called a superposition.

00:33:12.060 --> 00:33:16.280
Two level system of these
then it's considered a qubit.

00:33:16.280 --> 00:33:18.990
That's a certain kind of superposition,

00:33:18.990 --> 00:33:21.743
which we've talked about a lot,

00:33:22.740 --> 00:33:25.460
and any quantum system
when you measure it,

00:33:25.460 --> 00:33:27.300
it decides what state it's in,

00:33:27.300 --> 00:33:29.500
it collapses into a state
with a certain value

00:33:29.500 --> 00:33:31.170
of the thing you measured.

00:33:31.170 --> 00:33:32.660
The probability of each state

00:33:32.660 --> 00:33:35.990
is determined by what the
initial superposition was.

00:33:35.990 --> 00:33:37.903
So it won't always be the same thing.

00:33:39.900 --> 00:33:41.560
It's contradictory to assume

00:33:41.560 --> 00:33:43.780
it always was in the state you measured

00:33:43.780 --> 00:33:44.930
before you measured it.

00:33:44.930 --> 00:33:47.080
That's the toughest
part of it uncertainly.

00:33:48.050 --> 00:33:51.930
So this physicist Alexey Korotkov said,

00:33:51.930 --> 00:33:54.080
in quantum mechanics you
don't see what you get,

00:33:54.080 --> 00:33:55.203
you get what you see.

00:33:56.209 --> 00:34:01.209
So finally, qubits can be in
this perfectly correlated state

00:34:01.470 --> 00:34:04.150
while still behaving individually random,

00:34:04.150 --> 00:34:07.750
and this is an example
of entanglement, okay,

00:34:07.750 --> 00:34:09.550
and the state looks like that

00:34:09.550 --> 00:34:11.173
or in coins it looks like that.

00:34:14.740 --> 00:34:16.180
All right, so we're good here, right?

00:34:16.180 --> 00:34:17.893
We've mastered quantum mechanics.

00:34:19.629 --> 00:34:20.667
- [Florian] We've done it though.

00:34:20.667 --> 00:34:21.760
- We've done it.

00:34:21.760 --> 00:34:23.573
Okay, test tomorrow.

00:34:24.480 --> 00:34:25.693
Come to this link.

00:34:26.790 --> 00:34:29.970
So we've covered most
of the hard stuff, okay?

00:34:29.970 --> 00:34:32.130
And now we're gonna
understand quantum computing

00:34:32.130 --> 00:34:34.370
quantum information a little bit.

00:34:34.370 --> 00:34:39.290
So much later in 1927 there
was the fifth Solvay Conference

00:34:39.290 --> 00:34:41.090
and Einstein was there

00:34:41.090 --> 00:34:43.620
looking like the more familiar Einstein.

00:34:43.620 --> 00:34:47.340
There's Bohr, there's
Heisenberg, there's Schrodinger,

00:34:47.340 --> 00:34:51.100
and then there's this guy
whose name was Paul Dirac.

00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:54.180
Paul Dirac actually
right after Heisenberg,

00:34:54.180 --> 00:34:58.140
kind of put quantum mechanics
in an extremely elegant form

00:34:58.140 --> 00:34:59.370
and predicted new things

00:35:00.698 --> 00:35:02.440
that other people hadn't figured out

00:35:02.440 --> 00:35:05.253
and was generally
considered to be a genius,

00:35:06.380 --> 00:35:11.110
and he said in 1929 that
the underlying physical laws

00:35:11.110 --> 00:35:13.100
necessary for the mathematical theory

00:35:13.100 --> 00:35:14.840
of a large part of physics

00:35:14.840 --> 00:35:18.300
and the whole of chemistry
are thus completely known.

00:35:18.300 --> 00:35:19.980
This is a famous quote.

00:35:19.980 --> 00:35:23.630
The rest of the quote is often not given,

00:35:23.630 --> 00:35:25.560
and he says, the difficulty

00:35:25.560 --> 00:35:28.760
is only that the exact
application of these laws

00:35:28.760 --> 00:35:32.480
leads to equations much too
complicated to be soluble.

00:35:32.480 --> 00:35:34.120
It therefore becomes desirable

00:35:34.120 --> 00:35:36.870
that practical methods of
applying quantum mechanics

00:35:36.870 --> 00:35:38.643
should be developed.

00:35:39.830 --> 00:35:41.670
Well, for the next 50 years,

00:35:41.670 --> 00:35:44.700
quantum mechanics turned out
to be the theory of everything

00:35:44.700 --> 00:35:46.750
and nobody really wanted to worry

00:35:46.750 --> 00:35:49.960
about these foundational
issues and philosophical issues

00:35:49.960 --> 00:35:53.660
and there was this acronym
SUAC, shut up and calculate,

00:35:53.660 --> 00:35:58.320
which was enforced when I
started my PhD, for sure.

00:35:58.320 --> 00:36:00.010
So you weren't supposed to
worry about these things

00:36:00.010 --> 00:36:01.710
if you were serious about physics.

00:36:03.150 --> 00:36:08.150
Now, but the dirty secret
was we know the equations

00:36:08.440 --> 00:36:11.760
that would describe many complex systems

00:36:11.760 --> 00:36:15.420
of atoms and molecules or
even subatomic particles,

00:36:15.420 --> 00:36:18.480
but we often can't solve them, okay?

00:36:18.480 --> 00:36:20.040
And this is due to something

00:36:20.040 --> 00:36:22.900
that I call the the exponential wall.

00:36:22.900 --> 00:36:26.040
Now, because of the pandemic

00:36:26.040 --> 00:36:29.320
people are familiar
with exponential curve.

00:36:29.320 --> 00:36:31.280
Like you infect one person

00:36:31.280 --> 00:36:36.130
and each person there infects one more

00:36:36.130 --> 00:36:39.150
so two turns into four into
six, et cetera, et cetera,

00:36:39.150 --> 00:36:41.183
and it grows very quickly.

00:36:43.380 --> 00:36:46.110
So it turns out that the
equations of quantum mechanics

00:36:46.110 --> 00:36:47.810
are sort of like that,

00:36:47.810 --> 00:36:51.810
in the sense that as the
system gets more complicated,

00:36:51.810 --> 00:36:53.480
first, with the simple system

00:36:53.480 --> 00:36:55.930
we can actually solve
it with pencil and paper

00:36:55.930 --> 00:36:58.400
and give exams to undergraduates

00:36:58.400 --> 00:37:01.180
expecting them to be able to do that,

00:37:01.180 --> 00:37:02.870
and then as it gets more complicated

00:37:02.870 --> 00:37:05.570
we have two atoms or three

00:37:05.570 --> 00:37:07.633
then we turn to a computer,

00:37:08.680 --> 00:37:11.720
but if we really try to
solve them exactly for,

00:37:11.720 --> 00:37:14.730
I don't know, any medium-sized molecule,

00:37:14.730 --> 00:37:19.730
and we we do that using a
ordinary digital computer

00:37:20.200 --> 00:37:23.910
there is a brute force way
to solve these equations.

00:37:23.910 --> 00:37:26.180
They're called partial
differential equations,

00:37:26.180 --> 00:37:27.490
but we find

00:37:27.490 --> 00:37:30.840
that as the number of atoms
and molecules increase,

00:37:30.840 --> 00:37:32.930
the time that it's gonna take our computer

00:37:32.930 --> 00:37:37.290
to crank out the answer is growing

00:37:37.290 --> 00:37:38.640
and pretty soon we're estimating

00:37:38.640 --> 00:37:41.090
that's gonna take the history of mankind

00:37:41.090 --> 00:37:43.280
and then the history
of the earth and so on,

00:37:43.280 --> 00:37:46.150
and so there's no way
to directly solve those

00:37:46.150 --> 00:37:51.150
that we know prior to quantum
computing in human timescales.

00:37:51.470 --> 00:37:54.180
Now, in many cases we
found approximate methods

00:37:54.180 --> 00:37:55.770
that avoid the exponential wall

00:37:55.770 --> 00:37:57.420
and seem to agree with nature.

00:37:57.420 --> 00:37:58.970
So we've made a lot of progress,

00:37:58.970 --> 00:38:02.820
but in the cases where we
don't have a good approximation

00:38:02.820 --> 00:38:04.520
we can't predict anything.

00:38:04.520 --> 00:38:06.860
So that is the dirty secret

00:38:06.860 --> 00:38:10.610
and this really describes
many of the frontier problems

00:38:10.610 --> 00:38:11.810
in theoretical physics

00:38:11.810 --> 00:38:14.760
that we're trying to get
around the exponential wall.

00:38:14.760 --> 00:38:19.630
Okay, now, getting back now
to quantum information, okay?

00:38:19.630 --> 00:38:21.730
One of the few famous physicist

00:38:21.730 --> 00:38:24.270
who was involved with the
quantum information fringe

00:38:24.270 --> 00:38:25.660
was Richard Feynman,

00:38:25.660 --> 00:38:27.810
I hope many of you have heard of him,

00:38:27.810 --> 00:38:30.900
and in 1981, actually, I was there,

00:38:30.900 --> 00:38:32.860
but I didn't know about this conference,

00:38:32.860 --> 00:38:36.120
there was a conference on
the physics of computing

00:38:36.120 --> 00:38:39.070
and Feynman came and
gave the keynote address

00:38:39.070 --> 00:38:40.500
such as it was,

00:38:40.500 --> 00:38:45.360
and he said, okay, now
that I'm blocking here

00:38:45.360 --> 00:38:47.370
actually the quote, but you can see it.

00:38:47.370 --> 00:38:51.990
Can you simulate nature with a
new kind of quantum computer,

00:38:51.990 --> 00:38:53.100
it's not a Turing machine,

00:38:53.100 --> 00:38:54.870
it's a machine of a different kind

00:38:54.870 --> 00:38:56.750
with quantum computer elements.

00:38:56.750 --> 00:38:58.510
Meaning the hardware itself

00:38:58.510 --> 00:39:01.260
was in a quantum state of superposition,

00:39:01.260 --> 00:39:03.023
or something like that,

00:39:04.100 --> 00:39:06.630
and so he says it's not a Turing machine,

00:39:06.630 --> 00:39:09.540
it's not what we now would
call a classical computer.

00:39:09.540 --> 00:39:11.830
Well, nature isn't classical, dammit,

00:39:11.830 --> 00:39:13.960
and if you want a simulation of nature

00:39:13.960 --> 00:39:15.350
you better make it quantum,

00:39:15.350 --> 00:39:17.210
and by golly it's a wonderful problem

00:39:17.210 --> 00:39:19.540
because it doesn't look too easy.

00:39:19.540 --> 00:39:20.960
So that's how Feynman talked

00:39:22.170 --> 00:39:25.010
and he realized that maybe

00:39:25.010 --> 00:39:27.770
this would get around
the exponential wall,

00:39:27.770 --> 00:39:31.430
but this seemed like a crazy idea, right?

00:39:31.430 --> 00:39:35.520
Because we've already told
you that quantum bits exists

00:39:35.520 --> 00:39:36.530
in an uncertain state.

00:39:36.530 --> 00:39:38.610
I haven't even told you
how fragile they are,

00:39:38.610 --> 00:39:39.540
but even if you have them,

00:39:39.540 --> 00:39:41.720
if they're in a superposition,

00:39:41.720 --> 00:39:45.110
they may randomly fall
into one state or another.

00:39:45.110 --> 00:39:47.560
So you can't even
reliably store your bits,

00:39:47.560 --> 00:39:52.473
why would you want something
that could be a zero or a one?

00:39:53.510 --> 00:39:56.570
So forget about processing
in some predictable way

00:39:56.570 --> 00:39:57.600
to solve a problem.

00:39:57.600 --> 00:40:00.040
You are introducing noise
and loss of information

00:40:00.040 --> 00:40:01.570
at the basic hardware level,

00:40:01.570 --> 00:40:02.720
how could that be good?

00:40:04.050 --> 00:40:05.770
But as these people thought,

00:40:05.770 --> 00:40:09.410
what if quantum weirdness
is a feature and not a bug?

00:40:09.410 --> 00:40:11.460
And the basic idea of quantum simulation

00:40:11.460 --> 00:40:14.030
is that if the components of your computer

00:40:14.030 --> 00:40:16.150
can keep their quantumness

00:40:16.150 --> 00:40:18.600
and are hooked up and are programmed

00:40:18.600 --> 00:40:22.340
so that they have the same
kinds of interactions and forces

00:40:22.340 --> 00:40:23.900
as the atoms in a molecule,

00:40:23.900 --> 00:40:28.900
say that you're trying to
calculate the energy levels of,

00:40:28.970 --> 00:40:31.170
then just by running your computer

00:40:31.170 --> 00:40:33.930
there will be a way to
extract the energy level

00:40:33.930 --> 00:40:37.850
that you can't actually
do with a normal computer,

00:40:37.850 --> 00:40:40.440
just as this is just a
quantum analog simulator.

00:40:40.440 --> 00:40:42.090
Now, I must hasten to say

00:40:42.090 --> 00:40:44.130
that none of our computers before

00:40:44.130 --> 00:40:47.600
ever had quantum components in this sense.

00:40:47.600 --> 00:40:49.610
Okay, so if you did this

00:40:49.610 --> 00:40:52.610
it turns out there would
be no exponential wall

00:40:52.610 --> 00:40:53.443
and you would be able

00:40:53.443 --> 00:40:57.113
to treat much larger
quantum systems predictably.

00:40:58.310 --> 00:40:59.950
So throughout the '80s,

00:40:59.950 --> 00:41:03.270
scientists explored the
properties of quantum information

00:41:03.270 --> 00:41:04.780
and interesting things were found.

00:41:04.780 --> 00:41:07.690
You can find the history
there on Wikipedia,

00:41:07.690 --> 00:41:11.970
but really the big moment was in 1994.

00:41:14.530 --> 00:41:17.380
So 1994 Peter Shor,

00:41:17.380 --> 00:41:20.960
who was an applied mathematician
and computer scientist

00:41:20.960 --> 00:41:23.173
at Bell Labs at the time,

00:41:24.760 --> 00:41:29.760
he proved he was looking at this,

00:41:30.370 --> 00:41:33.790
and quantum calculations aren't
the only exponential walls.

00:41:33.790 --> 00:41:36.230
So as an applied mathematician
in computer science,

00:41:36.230 --> 00:41:38.550
he was interested in
other exponential walls

00:41:38.550 --> 00:41:40.080
and he had heard about this idea

00:41:40.080 --> 00:41:41.773
of a potential quantum computer,

00:41:42.780 --> 00:41:44.920
and he showed that a quantum computer

00:41:44.920 --> 00:41:47.720
could solve a computer
science math problem

00:41:47.720 --> 00:41:50.550
with an exponential wall,

00:41:50.550 --> 00:41:53.030
which was believed to
have an exponential wall.

00:41:53.030 --> 00:41:54.040
So in other words,

00:41:54.040 --> 00:41:56.320
it wasn't just a way to
simulate quantum mechanics

00:41:56.320 --> 00:41:58.280
and do physical science calculations,

00:41:58.280 --> 00:42:02.380
it was also a way to solve
problems in Applied Math

00:42:02.380 --> 00:42:06.210
that could apply to finance
to cryptography, et cetera,

00:42:06.210 --> 00:42:08.750
where exponential walls are everywhere

00:42:08.750 --> 00:42:11.233
in networking and
communications and so on.

00:42:12.240 --> 00:42:16.500
Here's an example of a non
physics exponential wall

00:42:16.500 --> 00:42:17.970
that probably you can relate to.

00:42:17.970 --> 00:42:21.610
This is somebody's estimate
of the days of computer time

00:42:21.610 --> 00:42:24.920
it would take to crack a
password of different length

00:42:24.920 --> 00:42:26.250
in terms of letters.

00:42:26.250 --> 00:42:28.630
So your desktop could
do up to five letters

00:42:28.630 --> 00:42:30.860
and then it's gonna
start taking much longer

00:42:30.860 --> 00:42:32.260
than you're willing to wait.

00:42:33.349 --> 00:42:35.450
A graphical processing unit,

00:42:35.450 --> 00:42:39.120
which is very fast for
gaming, it can go up to six,

00:42:39.120 --> 00:42:41.600
and then an Amazon cloud cluster

00:42:41.600 --> 00:42:43.230
can go up to seven or eight,

00:42:43.230 --> 00:42:44.110
and it's very clear

00:42:44.110 --> 00:42:47.410
that if you have 20
letters in your password,

00:42:47.410 --> 00:42:48.670
you're never gonna get there.

00:42:48.670 --> 00:42:51.100
So this is nothing to do with physics.

00:42:51.100 --> 00:42:53.580
These are problems in information science

00:42:53.580 --> 00:42:54.740
or in cryptography, rather,

00:42:54.740 --> 00:42:59.000
that we want to know the answers to, okay.

00:42:59.000 --> 00:43:02.510
So Shor showed that if you
had an ideal quantum computer

00:43:02.510 --> 00:43:06.150
with perfect cubits and
other things perfect,

00:43:06.150 --> 00:43:08.940
you could break the standard
encryption technology

00:43:08.940 --> 00:43:12.323
which underlies web browsing
and e-commerce on the internet.

00:43:14.560 --> 00:43:18.710
So this was a moment
that shocked the world.

00:43:21.650 --> 00:43:23.893
Okay, so what am I talking about?

00:43:25.090 --> 00:43:27.560
So cryptographers like exponential walls,

00:43:27.560 --> 00:43:30.120
particularly if they're one-way walls,

00:43:30.120 --> 00:43:33.050
that is that one way it's
very hard to find the answer,

00:43:33.050 --> 00:43:35.453
but the other way when
you decrypt it's easy,

00:43:36.420 --> 00:43:39.440
and factoring large
numbers into their primes

00:43:39.440 --> 00:43:42.200
is a convenient one-way wall.

00:43:42.200 --> 00:43:44.640
Mathematicians and computer
scientists knew this

00:43:44.640 --> 00:43:47.800
and it's used in something
called RSA encryption,

00:43:47.800 --> 00:43:51.340
which is used to send your
credit card on the internet.

00:43:51.340 --> 00:43:53.410
Just to remind you what a prime number is

00:43:53.410 --> 00:43:55.283
and a little bit about them.

00:43:56.150 --> 00:44:00.160
So 20 is one times two
times two times five,

00:44:00.160 --> 00:44:02.060
a prime number, well, okay.

00:44:02.060 --> 00:44:05.423
21 is shown there, 22 is shown there,

00:44:07.090 --> 00:44:09.410
and a prime number is...

00:44:09.410 --> 00:44:11.923
I didn't show itself as
a factor, I should have.

00:44:13.430 --> 00:44:16.810
A prime number has no
factors, but one and itself,

00:44:16.810 --> 00:44:19.690
and I'm sure most of you
do remember that, I hope.

00:44:19.690 --> 00:44:22.370
Now you might think, well,
we kind of just look at this

00:44:22.370 --> 00:44:24.050
and we figure this out, it's true.

00:44:24.050 --> 00:44:26.603
We could do that for not too big numbers,

00:44:27.540 --> 00:44:29.490
but if they're really big numbers

00:44:29.490 --> 00:44:31.340
then it's not easy to do.

00:44:31.340 --> 00:44:33.503
So trying factoring this guy.

00:44:34.350 --> 00:44:37.580
So this is not a randomly
chosen number that I typed.

00:44:37.580 --> 00:44:41.050
This is RSA-2048.

00:44:41.050 --> 00:44:46.050
It's a 617 decimal digit,
2,048 bits integer,

00:44:48.890 --> 00:44:52.470
which is semi prime, I'll explain that.

00:44:52.470 --> 00:44:54.110
So it's not a prime number

00:44:54.110 --> 00:44:59.040
and it's one of the
largest longest numbers

00:44:59.040 --> 00:45:01.090
that they use in this encryption scheme

00:45:01.090 --> 00:45:02.350
that I'm gonna tell you about

00:45:02.350 --> 00:45:05.910
and they offered many years ago $200,000

00:45:05.910 --> 00:45:09.160
to factorize this number,

00:45:09.160 --> 00:45:11.530
and as Wikipedia says

00:45:11.530 --> 00:45:15.040
it may not be factorizable
for many years to come.

00:45:15.040 --> 00:45:18.610
So RSA-2048 has only two prime factors

00:45:18.610 --> 00:45:20.510
other than one and itself.

00:45:20.510 --> 00:45:24.783
So p times q multiplied together
gives us this long number,

00:45:24.783 --> 00:45:27.670
that it's very hard to figure out p and q.

00:45:27.670 --> 00:45:30.460
Okay, and actually still

00:45:30.460 --> 00:45:33.900
no person or computer system
on earth can find that

00:45:33.900 --> 00:45:36.330
the factors p and q of this number

00:45:36.330 --> 00:45:38.730
except the people that constructed it,

00:45:38.730 --> 00:45:41.010
who hopefully are revealing it.

00:45:41.010 --> 00:45:44.130
So you could offer somebody
billions of dollars.

00:45:44.130 --> 00:45:47.340
This is an unsolvable problem, we believe,

00:45:47.340 --> 00:45:52.023
at the moment with the
finest techniques we know.

00:45:53.300 --> 00:45:56.097
So the team at RSA found p and q,

00:45:56.097 --> 00:45:57.870
likely to find large primes,

00:45:57.870 --> 00:45:59.480
and then they multiply them together,

00:45:59.480 --> 00:46:01.480
and there is a way to find large primes.

00:46:02.740 --> 00:46:04.640
So multiplying is easy, right?

00:46:04.640 --> 00:46:06.260
That you could even probably do

00:46:06.260 --> 00:46:07.530
although we could probably make a mistake

00:46:07.530 --> 00:46:08.680
with this long number,

00:46:08.680 --> 00:46:10.500
but a computer can do that easily,

00:46:10.500 --> 00:46:12.570
but figuring out the prime factors

00:46:12.570 --> 00:46:15.550
is believed to be exponentially hard

00:46:15.550 --> 00:46:17.180
and have an exponential wall.

00:46:17.180 --> 00:46:19.590
So there's a one-way
wall, it's easy to compute

00:46:19.590 --> 00:46:21.100
and knowing p and q

00:46:21.100 --> 00:46:23.630
it's quote unquote impossible,

00:46:23.630 --> 00:46:26.210
p and q, knowing it, okay,

00:46:26.210 --> 00:46:30.620
unless you have a quantum
computer post Shor, okay.

00:46:30.620 --> 00:46:32.690
Now the way this is often illustrated

00:46:32.690 --> 00:46:35.390
is we're going to send a message

00:46:35.390 --> 00:46:37.890
that we wanna encrypt
between Bob and Alice

00:46:37.890 --> 00:46:40.710
and each of those know the factors of N.

00:46:40.710 --> 00:46:42.020
I'm not even sure they both have to,

00:46:42.020 --> 00:46:44.130
but I'm just assuming that.

00:46:44.130 --> 00:46:47.310
Okay, this is Bob's credit card number.

00:46:47.310 --> 00:46:48.730
So he sends it

00:46:48.730 --> 00:46:53.730
by encoding it in a message of
bits that depend on N, okay?

00:46:54.610 --> 00:46:57.140
And he sends that message over

00:46:57.140 --> 00:47:00.000
and then knowing the factors of N

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:04.110
Alice can decode it and get
a Bob's credit card number,

00:47:04.110 --> 00:47:07.680
and if Eve is listening,
she's an eavesdropper,

00:47:07.680 --> 00:47:12.430
or has corrupted the security of the line,

00:47:12.430 --> 00:47:14.540
she will get an N

00:47:14.540 --> 00:47:16.610
but she won't be able to
find the prime factors

00:47:16.610 --> 00:47:18.210
and she won't be able to decode it,

00:47:18.210 --> 00:47:20.690
and this is literally one of the main ways

00:47:20.690 --> 00:47:22.040
that the internet allows you

00:47:22.040 --> 00:47:24.193
to send your credit card securely.

00:47:26.499 --> 00:47:28.300
So when Shor happened,

00:47:28.300 --> 00:47:32.230
cryptographer first braced
for a quantum revolution

00:47:32.230 --> 00:47:37.120
and by 2015 the US NSA
had revealed its intention

00:47:37.120 --> 00:47:40.460
to transition to quantum
resistant protocols,

00:47:40.460 --> 00:47:43.510
meaning that we won't use RSA,

00:47:43.510 --> 00:47:44.760
we'll find some other method

00:47:44.760 --> 00:47:46.640
that so far we haven't figured out

00:47:46.640 --> 00:47:49.910
if a quantum computer could break, okay?

00:47:49.910 --> 00:47:51.470
But we're still using it regularly

00:47:51.470 --> 00:47:53.360
and we don't have a quantum computer

00:47:53.360 --> 00:47:56.150
unless something very
surprising is out there

00:47:56.150 --> 00:47:58.873
that could break RSA at the moment, okay.

00:48:03.210 --> 00:48:06.250
So once the Shor algorithm
was invented, the race was on.

00:48:06.250 --> 00:48:11.250
I mean, intelligence,
privacy, e-commerce, finance,

00:48:12.780 --> 00:48:16.930
I mean, all these things care
about being able to crypt

00:48:16.930 --> 00:48:20.520
and encrypt and decrypt, and so on,

00:48:20.520 --> 00:48:22.730
and suddenly there was
this paradigm shift,

00:48:22.730 --> 00:48:25.400
quantum uncertainty
wasn't always a barrier

00:48:25.400 --> 00:48:26.980
to learning about nature.

00:48:26.980 --> 00:48:29.100
Actually, it could be the key

00:48:29.100 --> 00:48:33.063
to extracting previously
inaccessible knowledge,

00:48:34.090 --> 00:48:36.540
and you had to invent new hardware

00:48:36.540 --> 00:48:40.870
which would qualitatively
change the type of the problem.

00:48:40.870 --> 00:48:42.890
So before we thought

00:48:42.890 --> 00:48:45.010
that if a problem was exponentially hard

00:48:45.010 --> 00:48:46.070
on one type of computer,

00:48:46.070 --> 00:48:49.000
it would be exponentially
harder on any kind of computer,

00:48:49.000 --> 00:48:51.840
but according to Shor's
result that's not true,

00:48:51.840 --> 00:48:55.150
if you have the new
revolutionary quantum hardware

00:48:55.150 --> 00:49:00.150
this exponentially hard
problem could become solvable.

00:49:00.340 --> 00:49:03.740
Okay, now, it turns out
that all quantum speed up

00:49:03.740 --> 00:49:06.900
derives from superposition
and entanglement.

00:49:06.900 --> 00:49:09.210
So if nature wasn't like this

00:49:09.210 --> 00:49:10.620
we could never solve these problems,

00:49:10.620 --> 00:49:12.943
it would forever be outside of reach,

00:49:13.868 --> 00:49:15.100
and it uses everything

00:49:15.100 --> 00:49:16.880
Einstein hated about quantum mechanics.

00:49:16.880 --> 00:49:20.663
So there's quite a historical
pioneer irony there,

00:49:21.600 --> 00:49:23.980
and it's a new kind of machine,

00:49:23.980 --> 00:49:27.270
never previously attempted
in science and technology.

00:49:27.270 --> 00:49:30.840
So let me try to explain how
it could be, in some sense,

00:49:30.840 --> 00:49:32.320
so much more powerful,

00:49:32.320 --> 00:49:35.190
although this is a very
simplified illustration.

00:49:35.190 --> 00:49:38.800
So this is a conventional computer,

00:49:38.800 --> 00:49:43.790
a RAM chip with 100 billion bits on it

00:49:45.430 --> 00:49:47.480
and each bit has two states.

00:49:47.480 --> 00:49:49.780
So there are two to the N states

00:49:50.730 --> 00:49:54.010
that this chip could hold,

00:49:54.010 --> 00:49:57.070
two to the 100 billion is
an insanely large number.

00:49:57.070 --> 00:49:58.930
In fact, 300,

00:49:58.930 --> 00:50:03.010
two to the 300 is actually
an insanely large number.

00:50:03.010 --> 00:50:05.853
It's 10 to the 91 states.

00:50:06.770 --> 00:50:11.280
So if we were to explore
all states sequentially

00:50:11.280 --> 00:50:12.910
at one nanosecond,

00:50:12.910 --> 00:50:15.080
so we just turned on this chip

00:50:15.080 --> 00:50:17.160
and started with all zeros

00:50:17.160 --> 00:50:18.720
and then flipped one and then another,

00:50:18.720 --> 00:50:22.090
just did this once a nanosecond, okay,

00:50:22.090 --> 00:50:25.383
it would take greater than the
age of the universe, sorry.

00:50:27.342 --> 00:50:29.120
It would take longer
the age of the universe

00:50:29.120 --> 00:50:34.120
to just do this in a serial classical way,

00:50:35.990 --> 00:50:39.760
but in a quantum computer
would take less than a second.

00:50:39.760 --> 00:50:42.620
So we start with all zeros
in the quantum version

00:50:42.620 --> 00:50:44.000
and we don't flip one,

00:50:44.000 --> 00:50:46.040
we actually superposed it.

00:50:46.040 --> 00:50:49.810
We turn it into an equal
superposition of zero and one,

00:50:49.810 --> 00:50:52.720
which is something we
know how to do, okay?

00:50:52.720 --> 00:50:55.610
And then we do that for the
next one and the next one,

00:50:55.610 --> 00:50:58.260
and eventually when
we've done that 300 times

00:50:58.260 --> 00:51:03.260
we actually have all 10 to
the 91 terms in this big sum,

00:51:03.350 --> 00:51:07.300
all of which are now being
partially implemented

00:51:07.300 --> 00:51:11.070
in this 300 bit quantum register.

00:51:11.070 --> 00:51:11.903
So in that sense

00:51:11.903 --> 00:51:15.283
it does something completely
impossible classically,

00:51:17.141 --> 00:51:20.173
but very doable, quantum mechanically,

00:51:21.590 --> 00:51:24.840
and this would take
less than a millisecond

00:51:24.840 --> 00:51:26.100
actually, on a quantum computer.

00:51:26.100 --> 00:51:27.560
We think we don't have one yet,

00:51:27.560 --> 00:51:29.840
but so the quantum computer

00:51:29.840 --> 00:51:32.510
is the ultimate parallel processor,

00:51:32.510 --> 00:51:35.090
but it may also be the
most difficult technology

00:51:35.090 --> 00:51:37.530
we've ever tried to build.

00:51:37.530 --> 00:51:41.363
So why is it quantum computer
insanely difficult to build?

00:51:42.630 --> 00:51:45.730
Okay, classical bit is high or low voltage

00:51:45.730 --> 00:51:46.680
in a small circuit

00:51:46.680 --> 00:51:50.340
and the bit can just stay
there uncorrupted for years

00:51:50.340 --> 00:51:54.360
or a magnetic region and
a magnetic disk, whatever.

00:51:54.360 --> 00:51:56.670
The quantum bit is a real artificial atom

00:51:56.670 --> 00:51:58.970
with quantum levels, okay?

00:51:58.970 --> 00:52:01.890
So there's a real atom and
there's an artificial atom.

00:52:01.890 --> 00:52:04.120
This is one of the ones pioneered at Yale,

00:52:04.120 --> 00:52:06.200
where I have a superconducting structure

00:52:06.200 --> 00:52:07.630
that has energy levels,

00:52:07.630 --> 00:52:12.490
and can be treated as a kind
of artificial atom, okay,

00:52:12.490 --> 00:52:14.110
but the unprotected qubit,

00:52:14.110 --> 00:52:16.840
if I just leave it out in the environment

00:52:16.840 --> 00:52:20.733
it's going to just go away
in a nanosecond, okay.

00:52:21.890 --> 00:52:25.610
It just can't stay in some superposition,

00:52:25.610 --> 00:52:26.900
and the reason it can't

00:52:26.900 --> 00:52:30.550
is because being out in the
world is in an environment

00:52:30.550 --> 00:52:34.040
where atoms are bumping into,
where light is coming in,

00:52:34.040 --> 00:52:35.620
radiation is coming in,

00:52:35.620 --> 00:52:36.827
and it's measuring the qubit

00:52:36.827 --> 00:52:39.870
and it's making it decide
what state it's in,

00:52:39.870 --> 00:52:41.650
even though humans don't measure it,

00:52:41.650 --> 00:52:43.350
the environment is measuring it.

00:52:43.350 --> 00:52:47.100
So that's why quantum information
is incredibly fragile.

00:52:47.100 --> 00:52:49.860
This effect is called decoherence,

00:52:49.860 --> 00:52:52.550
and that's why we couldn't
have a real Schrodinger cat

00:52:52.550 --> 00:52:55.040
because anything big and living like a cat

00:52:55.040 --> 00:52:57.520
is interacting with the
environment to stay alive

00:52:57.520 --> 00:53:01.110
and so it not gonna be
in a superposition, okay?

00:53:01.110 --> 00:53:03.260
But so quantum information

00:53:03.260 --> 00:53:08.260
is like the most fragile kind
of information ever invented.

00:53:08.760 --> 00:53:10.280
Okay, well, that sounds hard,

00:53:10.280 --> 00:53:12.790
but can't we just shield our
qubits from the environment

00:53:12.790 --> 00:53:14.550
and keep them far apart from each other?

00:53:14.550 --> 00:53:17.130
I mean, we know how to
trap individual atoms,

00:53:17.130 --> 00:53:18.960
20, 30 years ago,

00:53:18.960 --> 00:53:20.660
we can cool things down.

00:53:20.660 --> 00:53:22.180
Can we just do that?

00:53:22.180 --> 00:53:24.190
Well, that's not enough, okay?

00:53:24.190 --> 00:53:28.413
Because you need to interact
your qubits to process.

00:53:29.350 --> 00:53:31.790
For example, you need
to create entanglement.

00:53:31.790 --> 00:53:35.500
So here's my qubit, one
that's in state one.

00:53:35.500 --> 00:53:37.680
Let's say I wanna put
it into a superposition.

00:53:37.680 --> 00:53:41.500
So insert it with some microwaves or laser

00:53:41.500 --> 00:53:44.220
and I put it into zero
one in a controlled way,

00:53:44.220 --> 00:53:46.630
and I have to bring my next qubit in,

00:53:46.630 --> 00:53:48.410
that's also in that superposition.

00:53:48.410 --> 00:53:49.490
No one entangled.

00:53:49.490 --> 00:53:52.460
So I wanna get rid of the
one zero, zero one state,

00:53:52.460 --> 00:53:54.060
so they have to talk to each other

00:53:54.060 --> 00:53:56.500
and eventually I'll end up
with the entangled state.

00:53:56.500 --> 00:54:00.670
So I had these interactions
that have to go perfectly.

00:54:00.670 --> 00:54:04.140
Any mistake, I'm not
gonna get the right state.

00:54:04.140 --> 00:54:05.410
So this is so difficult

00:54:05.410 --> 00:54:08.210
that we think we will need
error correction in hardware,

00:54:08.210 --> 00:54:10.560
which we do have in classical computers.

00:54:10.560 --> 00:54:12.960
We store one as three ones

00:54:12.960 --> 00:54:16.110
and then take the majority and so on,

00:54:16.110 --> 00:54:17.260
but it's much harder to do

00:54:17.260 --> 00:54:20.023
what possible to do in quantum mechanics.

00:54:21.330 --> 00:54:23.410
Now the current quantum computers,

00:54:23.410 --> 00:54:25.400
they're basically two main technologies.

00:54:25.400 --> 00:54:29.130
The one that was pioneered
here at Yale and other places

00:54:29.130 --> 00:54:31.460
involves the superconducting circuits

00:54:31.460 --> 00:54:33.570
that you cool down to
a hundredth of a degree

00:54:33.570 --> 00:54:35.083
above absolute zero,

00:54:36.110 --> 00:54:39.220
and then these artificial
atoms are on chips.

00:54:39.220 --> 00:54:42.000
They're little superconducting regions

00:54:42.000 --> 00:54:46.370
that are specially designed.

00:54:46.370 --> 00:54:49.640
This approach was
pioneered by my colleagues,

00:54:49.640 --> 00:54:52.470
Michelle Devoret, Steve
Girvin, and Rob Schoelkopf,

00:54:52.470 --> 00:54:54.530
I wanna shout out to them

00:54:54.530 --> 00:54:56.090
and this is called a transmon qubit

00:54:56.090 --> 00:54:59.560
which pretty much all the
industrial places are using them.

00:55:00.460 --> 00:55:02.920
Now the other approach
is to use real atoms

00:55:02.920 --> 00:55:07.320
and this blue thing here is
a string of trapped ions,

00:55:07.320 --> 00:55:08.780
so ionized atoms,

00:55:08.780 --> 00:55:11.040
which can be manipulated with laser beams

00:55:11.040 --> 00:55:13.433
and has to be kept in an old dry vacuum.

00:55:16.393 --> 00:55:21.393
So that's also extremely
fragile, but okay.

00:55:21.460 --> 00:55:23.610
So we immediately see
that quantum computers

00:55:23.610 --> 00:55:24.840
are not gonna be small,

00:55:24.840 --> 00:55:26.170
they're not gonna be portable,

00:55:26.170 --> 00:55:27.750
and they're not gonna be cheap,

00:55:27.750 --> 00:55:29.510
but they are going to...

00:55:30.630 --> 00:55:32.460
Because they have unique capability

00:55:32.460 --> 00:55:34.490
you can imagine that a quantum cloud

00:55:34.490 --> 00:55:39.320
would still be very, very
powerful and desirable.

00:55:39.320 --> 00:55:42.710
Now you might worry that
while it's pretty easy

00:55:42.710 --> 00:55:46.320
to think that a single
ion suspended in space

00:55:46.320 --> 00:55:50.480
is sort of not near its neighbors,

00:55:50.480 --> 00:55:52.090
stuff on a chip,

00:55:52.090 --> 00:55:54.530
where we have solids made of other atoms,

00:55:54.530 --> 00:55:57.020
you might say it's
probably quite difficult

00:55:57.020 --> 00:56:02.020
to make that type of
qubit avoid decoherence,

00:56:02.820 --> 00:56:04.490
and it was quite difficult.

00:56:04.490 --> 00:56:05.780
Back in the '90s

00:56:05.780 --> 00:56:08.730
when they were first
proposed and demonstrated,

00:56:08.730 --> 00:56:12.080
you had about a nanosecond
before they became useless

00:56:12.080 --> 00:56:15.840
and then over the last, what is it?

00:56:15.840 --> 00:56:17.510
Sort of 20ish years,

00:56:17.510 --> 00:56:20.830
there's been six order
of magnitude improvement

00:56:20.830 --> 00:56:23.670
so that we can store quantum
information on a chip

00:56:24.600 --> 00:56:29.040
and most of this improvement
was done by people at Yale.

00:56:29.040 --> 00:56:30.910
Also the world leader in
quantum error correction,

00:56:30.910 --> 00:56:32.570
which I don't have time to go into.

00:56:32.570 --> 00:56:34.910
So in the last three minutes here

00:56:34.910 --> 00:56:37.840
let me say something about
what quantum computing

00:56:37.840 --> 00:56:39.490
could do for mankind.

00:56:39.490 --> 00:56:41.810
So this is a picture of Einstein again.

00:56:41.810 --> 00:56:44.623
Gotta get him in there with
his friend, Fritz Haber,

00:56:45.622 --> 00:56:50.550
and Haber invented something
called the Haber-Bosch process

00:56:50.550 --> 00:56:52.660
in the beginning of the 20th Century,

00:56:52.660 --> 00:56:56.750
and it's a way to extract
nitrogen from the air

00:56:56.750 --> 00:56:58.030
and make ammonia.

00:56:58.030 --> 00:57:02.480
Ammonia is a very critical
component fertilizer

00:57:02.480 --> 00:57:04.670
and this led to the Green Revolution

00:57:04.670 --> 00:57:06.853
of the second half of the 20th Century,

00:57:08.030 --> 00:57:10.010
and it's actually a very tough bond

00:57:10.010 --> 00:57:11.530
to break this nitrogen bond.

00:57:11.530 --> 00:57:13.550
So it's very energy intensive

00:57:13.550 --> 00:57:17.640
and it has to be done
it 250 bars of pressure,

00:57:17.640 --> 00:57:19.810
which is the pressure

00:57:19.810 --> 00:57:22.080
two and a half kilometers under the sea,

00:57:22.080 --> 00:57:24.140
and at 450 degrees centigrade,

00:57:24.140 --> 00:57:26.910
which is the melting point of aluminum.

00:57:26.910 --> 00:57:29.550
So it's very energy intensive

00:57:29.550 --> 00:57:33.440
and it emits a lot of
CO2 when you do this.

00:57:33.440 --> 00:57:38.440
In fact, the National Academy of Sciences

00:57:39.160 --> 00:57:44.160
estimated that 1% of total
US energy for gas or oil,

00:57:47.960 --> 00:57:50.930
and what does it say net
energy and natural gas

00:57:50.930 --> 00:57:53.450
is used yearly, just for
the Haber-Bosch process.

00:57:53.450 --> 00:57:56.850
So 1% of all the energy
we use goes into this

00:57:56.850 --> 00:57:58.970
and creates a lot of CO2.

00:57:58.970 --> 00:58:01.430
So a grand challenge in quantum chemistry

00:58:01.430 --> 00:58:02.790
would be to figure out

00:58:02.790 --> 00:58:06.110
how we could do this much
less energy intensively,

00:58:06.110 --> 00:58:09.730
and here's this co-factor called Fe-Mo Co

00:58:09.730 --> 00:58:13.340
which if you let bacteria have it,

00:58:13.340 --> 00:58:17.180
they can produce nitrogen from soil

00:58:17.180 --> 00:58:18.690
at room temperature and pressure

00:58:18.690 --> 00:58:22.083
instead of the pressure of the
bottom of the sea and so on,

00:58:23.000 --> 00:58:24.310
and we don't know how they do it.

00:58:24.310 --> 00:58:25.610
It's too complicated.

00:58:25.610 --> 00:58:29.700
We don't know enough about the
properties of this co-factor

00:58:29.700 --> 00:58:30.870
to figure out.

00:58:30.870 --> 00:58:31.750
If we did,

00:58:31.750 --> 00:58:36.320
maybe we could actually turn
it into industrial process

00:58:36.320 --> 00:58:39.310
and save an enormous amount of energy.

00:58:39.310 --> 00:58:41.420
Another example I like

00:58:41.420 --> 00:58:43.600
is something related
to biomedical science,

00:58:43.600 --> 00:58:46.940
and this case these guys Sels and Demler,

00:58:46.940 --> 00:58:48.930
they talked to medical biophysicists

00:58:48.930 --> 00:58:51.680
to ask what could a quantum
computer do for you.

00:58:51.680 --> 00:58:54.820
Since Demler seems to be
married to a biophysicist,

00:58:54.820 --> 00:58:56.703
this was probably relatively easy,

00:58:57.908 --> 00:59:02.430
and they talked about this
metabolic dark matter.

00:59:02.430 --> 00:59:04.490
So we all know about genomics

00:59:04.490 --> 00:59:05.323
and some people know

00:59:05.323 --> 00:59:10.160
about cataloging all the
proteins, proteomics,

00:59:12.000 --> 00:59:14.920
but then the next level of
functional things in the body

00:59:14.920 --> 00:59:16.520
are called metabolites,

00:59:16.520 --> 00:59:19.140
and they're 200,000 metabolites

00:59:19.140 --> 00:59:22.730
and we only know what
1% of them do and so on

00:59:22.730 --> 00:59:26.070
and so we'd like to know that,

00:59:26.070 --> 00:59:27.780
but the way we try to figure it out

00:59:27.780 --> 00:59:31.920
was by shining NMR radiation on them

00:59:31.920 --> 00:59:34.110
and looking at the signal we get back,

00:59:34.110 --> 00:59:36.430
and this is very sparse information,

00:59:36.430 --> 00:59:37.820
and from this signal

00:59:37.820 --> 00:59:41.650
it's very hard to figure out
a complicated structure, okay?

00:59:41.650 --> 00:59:44.530
And so this is how we're doing it now,

00:59:44.530 --> 00:59:46.986
but we can't do it that well

00:59:46.986 --> 00:59:51.780
and Sels and his co-workers
designed an algorithm

00:59:51.780 --> 00:59:54.130
which uses a combination
of pattern recognition

00:59:55.202 --> 00:59:57.920
and AI and quantum simulation

00:59:57.920 --> 01:00:00.103
to identify metabolites from spectrum,

01:00:01.593 --> 01:00:03.970
and it used the Shor-like speed up.

01:00:03.970 --> 01:00:08.520
The same thing that Shor used
to speed up factorization

01:00:08.520 --> 01:00:10.540
and they did some examples

01:00:10.540 --> 01:00:14.420
and they were able to
reproduce known spectra.

01:00:14.420 --> 01:00:15.970
You might say, well, how did they do that

01:00:15.970 --> 01:00:18.070
'cause they don't have a quantum computer?

01:00:18.070 --> 01:00:20.010
And the answer is they only did it

01:00:20.010 --> 01:00:21.970
up to the exponential wall

01:00:21.970 --> 01:00:23.150
and they're just hoping

01:00:23.150 --> 01:00:24.230
if we get a quantum computer

01:00:24.230 --> 01:00:26.170
it can go through the exponential wall,

01:00:26.170 --> 01:00:28.770
but they're checking
that the idea is working

01:00:28.770 --> 01:00:32.820
with a classical computer
simulating a quantum computer.

01:00:32.820 --> 01:00:37.820
Okay, so that's all I wanted to get across

01:00:41.040 --> 01:00:45.670
about how exciting and
interesting the next phase is

01:00:45.670 --> 01:00:49.240
and how philosophically
and conceptually important

01:00:49.240 --> 01:00:51.693
the common information revolution is.

01:00:53.260 --> 01:00:56.150
So we're going boldly
into the quantum future

01:00:56.150 --> 01:01:01.150
and we at Yale hope to be
finding out what happens there.

01:01:02.820 --> 01:01:04.270
Thank you for your attention.

01:01:09.190 --> 01:01:11.670
- [Florian] There's a little
visual typing happening.

01:01:11.670 --> 01:01:14.157
Thank you so much for this talk doc.

01:01:15.530 --> 01:01:16.790
We have a question from Annie.

01:01:16.790 --> 01:01:17.930
You talked a little bit

01:01:17.930 --> 01:01:20.620
about what quantum computer could do.

01:01:20.620 --> 01:01:23.260
Can you tell us for...

01:01:23.260 --> 01:01:25.610
The question is what
kind of complex problem

01:01:25.610 --> 01:01:27.943
can quantum computer have solved so far?

01:01:29.716 --> 01:01:32.540
- Okay, maybe I didn't make this clear.

01:01:32.540 --> 01:01:35.400
So there aren't quantum computers really.

01:01:35.400 --> 01:01:38.330
I mean, they're very,
very kind of rudimentary

01:01:38.330 --> 01:01:41.040
and people are using them,

01:01:41.040 --> 01:01:46.040
but at the moment we
have dozens of qubits,

01:01:49.590 --> 01:01:51.950
not even 100 that work together,

01:01:51.950 --> 01:01:55.710
they don't work together
flexibly and so on.

01:01:55.710 --> 01:01:58.730
So there is no quantum computer

01:01:58.730 --> 01:02:01.090
that's really doing stuff yet.

01:02:01.090 --> 01:02:02.750
That's groundbreaking stuff.

01:02:02.750 --> 01:02:05.480
There are lots of little
examples of things they're doing.

01:02:05.480 --> 01:02:08.320
The first thing people
are doing is to try to,

01:02:08.320 --> 01:02:10.430
not the first thing but
one of the first things

01:02:10.430 --> 01:02:13.820
is to look at actual molecules

01:02:13.820 --> 01:02:15.610
because that's kind of
a very natural thing

01:02:15.610 --> 01:02:17.310
for quantum simulation

01:02:17.310 --> 01:02:19.390
and we have bigger quantum simulators

01:02:19.390 --> 01:02:21.600
and multipurpose computers.

01:02:21.600 --> 01:02:23.670
So that's the kind of things going on,

01:02:23.670 --> 01:02:26.760
but so far just looks really promising,

01:02:26.760 --> 01:02:28.443
nothing's been shown yet.

01:02:29.389 --> 01:02:31.094
- [Florian] And I guess
the question that I have

01:02:31.094 --> 01:02:34.500
and I have multiple regression
that is it doesn't exist yet,

01:02:34.500 --> 01:02:37.040
but like when do you think it will happen?

01:02:37.040 --> 01:02:37.873
- Well, I'm not--

01:02:37.873 --> 01:02:40.163
- [Florian] I think that's
the million dollar question.

01:02:41.100 --> 01:02:44.740
- So my colleague Rob Schoelkopf,

01:02:44.740 --> 01:02:46.420
I hope I quoted correctly,

01:02:46.420 --> 01:02:50.150
I think to have something

01:02:50.150 --> 01:02:53.020
that you really can learn how
to use the quantum computer

01:02:53.020 --> 01:02:54.590
and start doing things,

01:02:54.590 --> 01:02:56.780
which I'd say we're not
even quite there yet,

01:02:56.780 --> 01:03:00.033
that's gonna be only five
years maybe, maybe less.

01:03:02.890 --> 01:03:07.890
The breakthrough, the change
in human society, the economy,

01:03:08.640 --> 01:03:09.473
I don't know.

01:03:09.473 --> 01:03:11.680
I don't think it's either
gonna fail massively

01:03:11.680 --> 01:03:13.550
or it's gonna be here in 20 years,

01:03:13.550 --> 01:03:15.483
but that's just my gut feelings.

01:03:16.480 --> 01:03:18.930
There's been unbelievable progress made.

01:03:18.930 --> 01:03:20.960
So that's my gut feeling,

01:03:20.960 --> 01:03:22.730
but this is all over the map

01:03:22.730 --> 01:03:25.530
and a lot of people are
saying it's already here,

01:03:25.530 --> 01:03:26.610
we're already doing it,

01:03:26.610 --> 01:03:28.980
you can buy our web services.

01:03:28.980 --> 01:03:31.260
So you're gonna hear all the big spectrum

01:03:31.260 --> 01:03:34.060
of answers to that question.

01:03:37.227 --> 01:03:38.840
- [Florian] Are there any problem

01:03:38.840 --> 01:03:42.170
where even quantum computer
can hit the exponential wall

01:03:42.170 --> 01:03:43.720
you were talking about earlier?

01:03:44.740 --> 01:03:47.660
- Well, a partially answer to this.

01:03:47.660 --> 01:03:49.290
Well, first, yes,

01:03:49.290 --> 01:03:51.470
in the sense that we don't know

01:03:51.470 --> 01:03:54.230
the full capabilities
of a quantum computer,

01:03:54.230 --> 01:03:57.240
and so there are lots of
problems of exponential walls

01:03:57.240 --> 01:03:59.360
and we haven't figured
out a quantum algorithm

01:03:59.360 --> 01:04:01.370
to solve them fast,

01:04:01.370 --> 01:04:03.560
and I don't know whether
there's any proofs

01:04:03.560 --> 01:04:05.320
that they don't exist ones,

01:04:05.320 --> 01:04:09.040
but specifically, the new
kinds of coding problem

01:04:09.040 --> 01:04:10.590
your coding may wanna do,

01:04:10.590 --> 01:04:12.630
they're gonna use another math problem

01:04:12.630 --> 01:04:15.670
not factoring prime numbers, I'm sorry,

01:04:15.670 --> 01:04:17.620
finding prime factors of large numbers.

01:04:19.020 --> 01:04:21.320
They're gonna use a different
kind of hard math problem

01:04:21.320 --> 01:04:24.040
where nobody has come up
with a quantum algorithm

01:04:24.040 --> 01:04:27.590
and I think some people are
much further along than me

01:04:27.590 --> 01:04:31.460
in understanding which ones
can and cannot be solved,

01:04:31.460 --> 01:04:33.870
but certainly there are many
exponentially hard problems

01:04:33.870 --> 01:04:36.610
we haven't figured in quantum algorithms.

01:04:36.610 --> 01:04:39.167
Another thing I wanna mention is that,

01:04:39.167 --> 01:04:42.320
and this is now I'm quoting
a very knowledgeable person

01:04:42.320 --> 01:04:44.920
from Microsoft, Matthias Troyer,

01:04:44.920 --> 01:04:48.280
who said that quantum computing

01:04:48.280 --> 01:04:50.990
is gonna be good for
hard compute problems,

01:04:50.990 --> 01:04:52.930
not for big data problems.

01:04:52.930 --> 01:04:57.450
So if there's some place in the program

01:04:57.450 --> 01:04:59.240
or in the calculation,

01:04:59.240 --> 01:05:01.160
where there's an exponential wall,

01:05:01.160 --> 01:05:02.640
you'll use a quantum computer.

01:05:02.640 --> 01:05:05.370
If you wanna have
massive storage and so on

01:05:05.370 --> 01:05:06.713
and be able to use it,

01:05:08.200 --> 01:05:11.380
actually, you're gonna
use a classical computer

01:05:11.380 --> 01:05:13.390
that's gonna be easier in certain ways.

01:05:13.390 --> 01:05:16.263
So that was his general take on it.

01:05:17.670 --> 01:05:19.173
So networking and the kinds of things

01:05:19.173 --> 01:05:23.140
that Google wanna do is very
much a hard computer problem.

01:05:23.140 --> 01:05:24.620
- [Florian] I'm gonna
be talk about Google.

01:05:24.620 --> 01:05:27.270
That's a perfect segue
to the next question.

01:05:27.270 --> 01:05:29.960
Somebody is asking what did Google do

01:05:29.960 --> 01:05:33.570
with the quantum computer effort last year

01:05:33.570 --> 01:05:35.610
and the quantum supremacy?

01:05:35.610 --> 01:05:37.410
- So the,
- And how's it different?

01:05:39.030 --> 01:05:42.360
- The idea that emerged,

01:05:42.360 --> 01:05:44.090
which was a reasonable idea,

01:05:44.090 --> 01:05:49.090
was that well, let's find

01:05:53.200 --> 01:05:55.673
a problem which has an exponential wall,

01:05:56.840 --> 01:05:59.160
that may be the easiest problem

01:05:59.160 --> 01:06:01.350
to make quantum hardware for,

01:06:01.350 --> 01:06:04.170
and let's show that we can get an answer

01:06:04.170 --> 01:06:09.053
when no classical computer could.

01:06:10.310 --> 01:06:13.320
So it is not a useful problem

01:06:13.320 --> 01:06:14.730
and there's big arguments

01:06:14.730 --> 01:06:17.310
about whether it's even
an interesting problem.

01:06:17.310 --> 01:06:20.350
I heard both sides argued recently here

01:06:20.350 --> 01:06:21.663
in the Quantum Institute,

01:06:24.074 --> 01:06:26.763
but it wasn't a practical problem.

01:06:29.356 --> 01:06:33.650
So they did a kind of sorting problem,

01:06:33.650 --> 01:06:34.960
that's not even right,

01:06:34.960 --> 01:06:39.610
but it's essentially a statistical problem

01:06:39.610 --> 01:06:42.920
where you had to detect a
certain statistical behavior

01:06:42.920 --> 01:06:45.170
that was characteristic of interference

01:06:45.170 --> 01:06:46.257
in quantum mechanics

01:06:46.257 --> 01:06:48.230
and you could try to simulate that problem

01:06:48.230 --> 01:06:49.690
on a classical computer

01:06:49.690 --> 01:06:51.580
and they got to a size

01:06:51.580 --> 01:06:53.610
where no classical computer could do it

01:06:53.610 --> 01:06:54.750
and they got an answer

01:06:54.750 --> 01:06:59.390
which looked to be measuring
quantum interference.

01:06:59.390 --> 01:07:00.947
It was not dramatic (laughs).

01:07:03.130 --> 01:07:07.883
The quantum mechanics
predicted to and they got 1.06.

01:07:09.060 --> 01:07:11.910
So their computer was
not working perfectly,

01:07:11.910 --> 01:07:14.340
but they claimed that
in classical computer

01:07:14.340 --> 01:07:16.510
couldn't have even gotten the 0.06.

01:07:16.510 --> 01:07:18.867
So it was a very abstract problem

01:07:21.070 --> 01:07:25.733
that they wanted to
claim quantum supremacy.

01:07:26.640 --> 01:07:30.380
So, but I think the fact
that they got so close

01:07:30.380 --> 01:07:32.630
is showing that we're making progress

01:07:32.630 --> 01:07:35.090
and there are different approaches.

01:07:35.090 --> 01:07:36.350
Our approach here at Yale

01:07:36.350 --> 01:07:39.250
has been to try to do
quantum error correction

01:07:39.250 --> 01:07:42.680
and do much cleaner quantum computations

01:07:42.680 --> 01:07:45.210
with smaller numbers of bits and so on,

01:07:45.210 --> 01:07:47.223
and so that's the approach we've chosen.

01:07:48.160 --> 01:07:50.510
Google has chosen one approach,

01:07:50.510 --> 01:07:55.473
IBM is more or less going more
in our direction and so on.

01:07:56.590 --> 01:07:58.040
So yes, it was a big deal

01:07:58.040 --> 01:08:00.070
'cause it did show something

01:08:00.070 --> 01:08:03.773
beyond what a classical computer could do.

01:08:04.900 --> 01:08:06.500
So in that sense it's big,

01:08:06.500 --> 01:08:10.250
but it's a tiny, tiny
inch above the threshold.

01:08:10.250 --> 01:08:12.463
I mean, it's not anything dramatic yet.

01:08:17.960 --> 01:08:20.120
- [Florian] Somebody asked the question,

01:08:20.120 --> 01:08:23.630
I've noticed there's a lot of physicists

01:08:23.630 --> 01:08:26.720
working on these topics with
the background in physics,

01:08:26.720 --> 01:08:30.010
what are the possibility
for engineers in this field?

01:08:30.010 --> 01:08:35.010
- So from my understanding
from talking to Rob Schoelkopf,

01:08:35.140 --> 01:08:36.640
the director of this institute

01:08:36.640 --> 01:08:40.733
who founded the QCI, this startup company,

01:08:42.920 --> 01:08:45.230
oh, sorry, what do I need to do?

01:08:45.230 --> 01:08:50.230
Okay, sorry, is that actually
more than half the people

01:08:51.260 --> 01:08:52.740
or half the people are engineers

01:08:52.740 --> 01:08:54.923
and software engineers and so on.

01:08:55.820 --> 01:09:00.440
So I think it's very
clear that this is a field

01:09:00.440 --> 01:09:03.090
where we'll need lots
of electrical engineers,

01:09:03.090 --> 01:09:07.350
computer scientists, software engineers,

01:09:07.350 --> 01:09:09.180
probably mechanical engineers.

01:09:09.180 --> 01:09:12.860
Anyway, so there's a lot
of opportunity there,

01:09:12.860 --> 01:09:16.290
but the big barrier, the
exponential wall here,

01:09:16.290 --> 01:09:17.830
is that you have to understand something

01:09:17.830 --> 01:09:19.170
about quantum mechanics

01:09:19.170 --> 01:09:22.400
and so we're working
on educational courses

01:09:22.400 --> 01:09:25.890
that can take someone without
a full physics background

01:09:25.890 --> 01:09:28.240
and they could just use my talk,

01:09:28.240 --> 01:09:32.130
but it's probably not quite
deep enough to do that.

01:09:32.130 --> 01:09:34.650
So yes, there will be a
big role for engineers.

01:09:34.650 --> 01:09:35.709
- [Florian] And then we can also say

01:09:35.709 --> 01:09:36.840
that the Yale Quantum Institute

01:09:36.840 --> 01:09:39.940
regroups departments out here
in physics, applied physics,

01:09:39.940 --> 01:09:42.760
computer science, electrical
engineering, chemistry,

01:09:42.760 --> 01:09:45.370
so we're really trying to bring a very--

01:09:45.370 --> 01:09:48.610
- It's naturally interdisciplinary

01:09:48.610 --> 01:09:50.670
and also at least for the moment,

01:09:50.670 --> 01:09:55.250
it's like a seller's market.

01:09:55.250 --> 01:09:58.660
So anybody with quantum
training that's legit

01:09:58.660 --> 01:10:01.950
you can get hired maybe
without doing any postdoc

01:10:01.950 --> 01:10:03.310
or maybe out of your PhD,

01:10:03.310 --> 01:10:04.143
but we don't like that.

01:10:04.143 --> 01:10:05.463
So forget I said that.

01:10:07.800 --> 01:10:08.633
- [Florian] Thank you,

01:10:08.633 --> 01:10:10.793
there's a lot more question pouring in.

01:10:12.070 --> 01:10:15.130
What in particular do you see the YQI team

01:10:15.130 --> 01:10:17.290
contributing to the coming decades?

01:10:17.290 --> 01:10:20.150
Like, what's next, what do
you think that the Yale labs

01:10:20.150 --> 01:10:22.530
will bring in the field?

01:10:22.530 --> 01:10:23.646
- Thank you, I don't know.

01:10:23.646 --> 01:10:24.760
This is a Yale person.

01:10:24.760 --> 01:10:25.900
- [Florian] It's an anonymous thing.

01:10:25.900 --> 01:10:27.460
Maybe somebody from
Yale is sending us that,

01:10:27.460 --> 01:10:29.274
like a local question for us.

01:10:29.274 --> 01:10:33.000
- Okay, so I didn't
wanna be too parochial,

01:10:33.000 --> 01:10:35.360
but I think that,

01:10:35.360 --> 01:10:37.600
I mean Yale deserves a lot of credit,

01:10:37.600 --> 01:10:40.660
both for inventing a lot
of the foundational stuff,

01:10:40.660 --> 01:10:42.540
but for taking a completely different

01:10:42.540 --> 01:10:46.460
and very patient systematic approach,

01:10:46.460 --> 01:10:51.460
and in doing that they've
actually changed the paradigm

01:10:51.470 --> 01:10:55.120
and only a few other places.

01:10:55.120 --> 01:10:57.420
As of last year no one
but Yale was doing this.

01:10:57.420 --> 01:10:59.990
I understand now some other
people are doing this,

01:10:59.990 --> 01:11:04.990
and they stopped using the
superconducting circuits

01:11:05.330 --> 01:11:07.090
as their artificial atoms.

01:11:07.090 --> 01:11:11.610
They started actually using
photons, light in cavities,

01:11:11.610 --> 01:11:13.690
and I don't have a picture here,

01:11:13.690 --> 01:11:16.960
but it doesn't look like the flat thing

01:11:16.960 --> 01:11:20.183
that I have here, right?

01:11:21.150 --> 01:11:22.630
The new thing looks like something

01:11:22.630 --> 01:11:24.573
you might have on your cable box.

01:11:27.610 --> 01:11:30.280
Yeah, it doesn't look like that flat thing

01:11:30.280 --> 01:11:32.743
that I have in this picture.

01:11:34.170 --> 01:11:37.690
It's three dimensional and it's small,

01:11:37.690 --> 01:11:39.710
it's not as big as your cable box,

01:11:39.710 --> 01:11:43.023
but it looks very different,

01:11:44.200 --> 01:11:47.230
and that's 'cause we're
storing information in light

01:11:47.230 --> 01:11:49.960
and the reason we're storing
information in light,

01:11:49.960 --> 01:11:52.163
or in photons, actually,
microwave photons,

01:11:53.170 --> 01:11:56.550
is because photons don't interact strongly

01:11:56.550 --> 01:12:01.390
and they have fewer ways that
they can actually disappear,

01:12:01.390 --> 01:12:03.160
and so we've already shown

01:12:03.160 --> 01:12:07.000
that they can have 10
times longer lifetimes

01:12:07.000 --> 01:12:10.000
than the superconducting
qubits we used to use.

01:12:10.000 --> 01:12:13.010
So, and we hope to pioneer

01:12:13.010 --> 01:12:17.450
this method of using storage with photons

01:12:17.450 --> 01:12:20.840
and processing with
superconducting elements,

01:12:20.840 --> 01:12:25.760
which is unique to Yale and now
it's spreading a little bit,

01:12:25.760 --> 01:12:26.810
and then the second thing

01:12:26.810 --> 01:12:29.410
is that we hope to add error correction,

01:12:29.410 --> 01:12:32.530
efficient error correction
way before anybody else,

01:12:32.530 --> 01:12:36.810
and I won't tell you, it
gets pretty technical,

01:12:36.810 --> 01:12:39.430
why we think we are so far ahead,

01:12:39.430 --> 01:12:43.360
but we have good reasons to think this.

01:12:43.360 --> 01:12:46.070
- [Florian] We've gotten a
lot of question in the Q&amp;A

01:12:46.070 --> 01:12:48.750
regarding like the investment of China

01:12:48.750 --> 01:12:49.890
on quantum computing.

01:12:49.890 --> 01:12:53.877
They are wondering what are
the the implication of this,

01:12:53.877 --> 01:12:55.850
and if they will have gone a bit before,

01:12:55.850 --> 01:12:57.982
but I think you've answered
this question saying like,

01:12:57.982 --> 01:13:01.073
we hope that the Yale team has something.

01:13:02.870 --> 01:13:07.870
- Well, so I think that
the idea of encryption,

01:13:08.850 --> 01:13:13.850
and sending encrypted
messages is very exciting

01:13:14.450 --> 01:13:17.400
to the Chinese and lots
of people to be fair,

01:13:17.400 --> 01:13:21.030
and they've been really
pushing quantum communication,

01:13:21.030 --> 01:13:22.170
not quantum computing.

01:13:22.170 --> 01:13:25.343
I guess they're catching up
somewhat in quantum computing,

01:13:27.800 --> 01:13:29.930
and one of the things I didn't get to say

01:13:29.930 --> 01:13:32.830
is that there is a proof

01:13:32.830 --> 01:13:36.330
that you can send
unbreakable coded messages

01:13:36.330 --> 01:13:38.170
with quantum mechanics,

01:13:38.170 --> 01:13:40.320
and that is out there already

01:13:40.320 --> 01:13:42.370
and it's being sold commercially.

01:13:42.370 --> 01:13:45.950
We don't know who's using it in defense.

01:13:45.950 --> 01:13:49.910
So I think they're very much
interested in the secrecy,

01:13:49.910 --> 01:13:53.000
coding, decoding aspect of it

01:13:53.000 --> 01:13:54.477
more than the quantum
computing at the moment,

01:13:54.477 --> 01:13:56.453
but that's just a guess.

01:13:57.620 --> 01:13:58.900
- [Florian] I think the clock is running

01:13:58.900 --> 01:14:02.430
and I'm gonna maybe end on this question.

01:14:02.430 --> 01:14:03.710
Since we're a university

01:14:03.710 --> 01:14:05.933
we have a question from a college student.

01:14:07.369 --> 01:14:10.450
Willis asking like what
could a student do today

01:14:10.450 --> 01:14:12.330
to prepare for quantum
computing in the future?

01:14:12.330 --> 01:14:15.910
What are the subjects or
major you should consider?

01:14:15.910 --> 01:14:18.180
Is it better to do CS or physics?

01:14:18.180 --> 01:14:22.360
Like what is your take on a young person

01:14:22.360 --> 01:14:24.583
who wants to join the efforts?

01:14:27.420 --> 01:14:31.050
- I think that both are very good.

01:14:31.050 --> 01:14:33.750
So you should do what you like more,

01:14:33.750 --> 01:14:38.270
specifically, Shor who was not
trained in quantum mechanics,

01:14:38.270 --> 01:14:41.730
but just kind of understood
the math of quantum mechanics

01:14:41.730 --> 01:14:43.500
was able to find this thing

01:14:44.370 --> 01:14:47.120
that he knew stuff in computer science

01:14:47.120 --> 01:14:48.560
and in complexity theory

01:14:48.560 --> 01:14:50.950
that most physicists at
that time didn't know,

01:14:50.950 --> 01:14:54.090
and he made the revolution, honestly,

01:14:54.090 --> 01:14:56.210
so he started the revolution.

01:14:56.210 --> 01:15:01.210
So in that sense you can master the math

01:15:01.230 --> 01:15:03.090
and do something as a computer scientist

01:15:03.090 --> 01:15:04.573
or applied mathematician.

01:15:07.370 --> 01:15:10.550
Being a physicist if you
wanna work on hardware,

01:15:10.550 --> 01:15:12.760
that's definitely a good
idea to be a physicist,

01:15:12.760 --> 01:15:14.950
or if there are joint efforts

01:15:14.950 --> 01:15:18.650
between physics and engineering,
you could be an engineer.

01:15:18.650 --> 01:15:20.140
So that would be an answer,

01:15:20.140 --> 01:15:22.030
I think either are promising.

01:15:22.030 --> 01:15:24.670
- [Florian] There's also the
question he had it was like,

01:15:24.670 --> 01:15:27.410
he was worried about having his work

01:15:27.410 --> 01:15:29.160
and computer science being obsolete

01:15:30.556 --> 01:15:32.870
since the field is moving so fast,

01:15:32.870 --> 01:15:36.120
and so that was also one of the concern,

01:15:36.120 --> 01:15:37.170
and I think in that case

01:15:37.170 --> 01:15:40.510
people here working
really in collaboration.

01:15:40.510 --> 01:15:43.060
We've talked a lot with the
people in computer science here

01:15:43.060 --> 01:15:47.060
and so I think the field is
moving along and so yeah.

01:15:47.060 --> 01:15:49.230
- I also I sort of said this,

01:15:49.230 --> 01:15:50.410
but I said it fast,

01:15:50.410 --> 01:15:52.380
and maybe it's a good thing to end on.

01:15:52.380 --> 01:15:54.820
You're not gonna have a
quantum computer in your phone,

01:15:54.820 --> 01:15:57.400
you're not gonna have a
quantum computer in your house.

01:15:57.400 --> 01:15:59.810
You don't need a quantum
computer in your house,

01:15:59.810 --> 01:16:03.210
you're not always running up
against big compute problems

01:16:03.210 --> 01:16:05.300
with exponential walls.

01:16:05.300 --> 01:16:07.210
Google is running up against them

01:16:07.210 --> 01:16:08.850
when they're trying to route signals

01:16:08.850 --> 01:16:13.520
and do stuff over a
billion links and so on.

01:16:13.520 --> 01:16:16.620
So it's not a replacement

01:16:16.620 --> 01:16:19.100
for all of the classical
computing and software

01:16:19.100 --> 01:16:20.323
or anything like that.

01:16:21.830 --> 01:16:25.530
So clearly you're not
gonna be totally obsolete

01:16:25.530 --> 01:16:27.960
unless you're very interested
in complexity theory,

01:16:27.960 --> 01:16:31.370
and even then it won't
be obsolete, I suspect.

01:16:31.370 --> 01:16:32.760
- [Florian] I think we're
gonna end on that note.

01:16:32.760 --> 01:16:36.380
Thank you so much for tuning in.

01:16:36.380 --> 01:16:38.490
We have a lot of unanswered questions.

01:16:38.490 --> 01:16:39.990
They were actually very technical.

01:16:39.990 --> 01:16:42.420
So maybe in the future we'll
have another event like this

01:16:42.420 --> 01:16:45.370
for a slightly more technical
approach with the Q&amp;A system

01:16:45.370 --> 01:16:48.830
and maybe a moderate conversation.

01:16:48.830 --> 01:16:50.291
Thank you so much for tuning in.

01:16:50.291 --> 01:16:54.708
- We can bring in people
that know more than quantum.

01:16:55.720 --> 01:16:57.260
- [Florian] You should
consider subscribing

01:16:57.260 --> 01:16:58.570
to our public event newsletter

01:16:58.570 --> 01:17:01.574
that you can find on our website,

01:17:01.574 --> 01:17:05.850
quantuminstitute.yale.edu/subscribe.

01:17:05.850 --> 01:17:07.350
Thank you all so much,

01:17:07.350 --> 01:17:08.590
and thank you, Doug,

01:17:08.590 --> 01:17:12.690
for taking the time to tell us
all about quantum computers.

01:17:12.690 --> 01:17:13.640
Have a great night.

